; ======================================================================
; SDIO for the Raspberry Pi 4 - the bus the on-board WiFi part sits on.
; Library, not a compiler builtin.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; This drives the SECOND SD controller on the BCM2711 - the Arasan
; SDHCI block at ARM $FE300000 - and speaks SDIO to whatever is wired to
; it. On a Pi 4 Model B that is exactly one thing: the Cypress /
; Infineon CYW43455 combo radio, soldered down, on GPIO 34..39.
;
; IT IS NOT emmc.pi4 AND IT MUST NOT BECOME emmc.pi4. That file drives
; EMMC2 at $FE340000, which is the micro-SD socket. Two different
; controllers, two different pin groups, two different devices, and the
; only thing they share is that both are SDHCI-class. Nothing in this
; file touches $FE340000 and nothing in emmc.pi4 touches $FE300000.
;
; ======================================================================
; USE - this file names no other file (house rule: a library never
; includes a library). The MAIN program lists what it needs:
;
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"     ; to PRINT the reason
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"  ; REQUIRED - see below
;     XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/sdio.pi4"
;
;     MailboxInit()
;     SdioSetBaseClockKhz(...)      ; see THE BASE CLOCK below
;     ; WL_ON is no longer the caller's job - see THE POWER RAIL below.
;
;     If SdioInit() = 0
;       PutS(SdioErrorText())
;       PutS(" cmd=") : PutHex2(SdioLastCmd())
;       PutS(" intr=") : PutHex8(SdioLastIntr())
;       PutS(" state=") : PutHex8(SdioLastStatus())
;     EndIf
;
; MAILBOX.PI4 IS NOW A HARD REQUIREMENT AND IT DID NOT USED TO BE.
; Until 2026-08-26 this file touched nothing but memory-mapped registers,
; and asserting WL_ON was left to whoever included it. The board then
; ran the diagnostic for the first time and said that the one step this
; file did NOT own - the radio's enable pin - is the step that decides
; whether CMD5 has anywhere to land. Owning half of a bring-up produces
; a driver that succeeds at every stage it can see and finds nothing.
;
; So SdioInit() now asserts WL_ON itself, and asks the firmware about
; the EMMC clock and the SD power device, before it forms any opinion
; about the controller. It does that through the property mailbox's
; PUBLIC ESCAPE HATCH - MailboxBegin / MailboxSetWord / MailboxSend /
; MailboxWord - and adds no wrapper, because mailbox.pi4 is another
; worker's file today and because that file's own header (see its
; ESCAPE HATCH section) says plainly that an unwrapped tag is the
; caller's to issue. NOTHING HERE EDITS mailbox.pi4.
;
; The precedent for one library calling a sibling's public procedures
; without including it is clocks.pi4, which calls MailboxClockRate() and
; MailboxClockRateMeasured() exactly this way.
;
; IF THE MAILBOX IS NOT READY NONE OF IT IS FATAL. Every mailbox step is
; guarded by MailboxReady(), records its own outcome, and carries on. A
; caller that never calls MailboxInit() gets the old behaviour and a set
; of accessors that say "not attempted" rather than "failed".
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT HAS AND HAS NOT RUN ON SILICON. READ THIS FIRST.
; ======================================================================
; THIS FILE CARRIES THE CYW43455. Everything the Wi-Fi radio has ever
; done on this board went through the transport in this file, and the
; radio has been scanned, associated and read back from. The section
; below used to say the first probe run "IS THE ONLY MEASUREMENT THIS
; FILE HAS"; that was written on 2026-08-26 at about midday and was
; overtaken the same evening. It is kept, in order, because the ladder
; from a refusal to an association is the useful record.
;
; ---- WHAT IS PROVEN, IN THE ORDER IT WAS PROVEN ---------------------
;
;   2026-08-26 evening - CMD53 BLOCK MODE, and both bugs were ours.
;   From the board, `pi4WifiUp`/`pi4SdioProbe`:
;
;       byte-mode max              64      the number that was wrong
;       card can multi-block        1      CCCR CAPS bit SMB
;       byte-mode read (want 1)     1      THE CONTROL. Previously 0.
;       control mismatches          0      and not "NOT COMPARED"
;       block-mode read (want 1)    1      bytes compared 256, 0 bad
;
;   So a CMD5 HAS been answered, an OCR busy bit HAS set, an RCA HAS
;   been published, CCCR bytes HAVE been read back, and 256 bytes HAVE
;   crossed the Buffer Data Port in one block-mode transfer. Every item
;   on the old "STILL NEVER OBSERVED" list below is now observed.
;
;   The two defects were both this file's:
;     * 512 IS THE PROTOCOL'S BYTE-MODE CEILING, NOT THE FUNCTION'S.
;       Linux takes the minimum of three bounds
;       (v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_io.c:189-202): the host's max_blk_size
;       (512), the FUNCTION's current block size (64), and a flat 512.
;       Sixty-four. Every CMD53 this project had ever issued before was
;       a 4-byte backplane register read, so the bound had never been
;       reachable - the caller that used a length nobody had tried is
;       what exposed it, not the refactor.
;     * AN R5 REFUSAL LEFT THE DATA LINE INHIBITED. sdio_CheckR5 had
;       returned without resetting DAT since the file was written and
;       had never fired, because no card had ever been handed a CMD53 it
;       was willing to refuse. The controller reported Command Complete
;       with no error bit, so nothing reset anything; DAT Inhibit stayed
;       asserted and every later command died of SDIO_ERR_INHIBIT with
;       `intr $00000000`. Fixed in sdio_Cmd53Xfer. THE REFUSAL IS THE
;       FAULT AND THE NEVER-STARTED COMMAND IS THE WRECKAGE - a stuck
;       bus makes every subsequent symptom a lie, including
;       Cyw43PrepareDownload's "32-bit backplane read failed" two files
;       away.
;
;   2026-08-26 late - 609,309 BYTES OF FIRMWARE ACROSS THIS TRANSPORT,
;   verified byte for byte against a read-back, NVRAM cooked from 2074
;   to 1748 bytes and placed at the top of the radio's RAM, and the
;   CYW43455's ARM released:
;
;       EMMC clock Hz (id 1)  250000000    base clock kHz  250000
;       SdioInit / enable F1 / Cyw43Attach     1 1 1
;       block mode on                          1
;       Cyw43UploadFirmware / UploadNvram      1 1
;       Cyw43Start                             1
;       ram base $00198000   ram size 819200 ($C8000)
;
;   2026-08-27 - the radio SCANNED (two networks, five escan records)
;   and ASSOCIATED on 2.4 GHz, with four read-backs against a baseline
;   taken before the join: BSSID 04:42:1A:A5:BC:90 byte for byte,
;   SSID, channel 2, RSSI -35 dBm. Every one of those bytes travelled
;   through this file.
;
; ---- SdioInit NEEDS A CLOCK FROM THE MAILBOX AND SAYS SO ------------
;
; On a BCM2711 the SDHCI Capabilities register reads back ZERO for the
; base-clock field, so the controller cannot be asked how fast it is
; fed; only the VideoCore firmware knows. CLOCK ID 1 IS EMMC, NOT
; EMMC2 - different clocks feeding different controllers. Getting it
; wrong does not refuse; it yields a plausible number and a bus at the
; wrong speed, which reads as bad silicon.
;
; ---- THE FIRST RUN, kept because it is where the address was settled -
;
; Written 2026-08-25 with no board. On 2026-08-26 the bench ran
; RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4SdioProbe.pi4 for the first time
; and it REFUSED:
;
;     mailbox init (want 1)     1
;     WL_ON asserted (want 1)   0
;     EMMC clock Hz (id 1)      250000000
;     SdioInit (want 1)         0
;     sdio: Capabilities register all-zero or all-ones - wrong device?
;     last cmd $FF  last intr $00000000  last state $000F0001
;
; and then, read by hand from the monitor prompt:
;
;     SDIO  $FE300000 (base+00) = $00000000
;     SDIO  $FE300040 (CAPS)    = $00000000
;     SDIO  $FE300044 (CAPS_HI) = $00000000
;     SDIO  $FE3000FC           = $99020000   <- HOST VERSION ANSWERS
;     EMMC2 $FE340000 (base+00) = $00000000
;     EMMC2 $FE340040 (CAPS)    = $45EE6432   <- the neighbour is fine
;     EMMC2 $FE3400FC           = $10020000
;     SDHOST $FE202000          = $00000000
;
; SO THE ADDRESS IS RIGHT AND THE BLOCK IS DECODING. $FE3000FC is the
; word holding Slot Interrupt Status at $FC and Host Controller Version
; at $FE; the top half is $9902, which is specification version 2 -
; SDHCI 3.00 - and vendor version $99. Two independent facts fall out of
; that single word: reads of this window reach real silicon, and the
; silicon they reach identifies itself as an SDHCI host. The EMMC2 line
; is the control: the same code, the same translation, $40000 higher, a
; populated Capabilities register. NOBODY SHOULD EVER AGAIN GO LOOKING
; AT THE BASE ADDRESS OR AT THE LOW-PERIPHERAL TRANSLATION BECAUSE OF A
; ZERO CAPABILITIES REGISTER, and the paragraph after next is why.
;
; ~~STILL NEVER OBSERVED, not once: a CMD5 answered, an OCR busy bit~~
; ~~setting, an RCA published, a CCCR byte read back, a byte crossing~~
; ~~the Buffer Data Port.~~
;
; ALL FIVE WERE OBSERVED THE SAME EVENING. That list is struck through
; rather than deleted because it is the exact shape of what a first
; silicon run leaves open, and it was answered in one session - a
; reminder that this kind of list expires fast and must be dated.
;
; ---- WHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN ---------------------------------------
;
;   * A COLD POWER-ON OF THE RADIO. `WL_ON asserted 1` is in the good
;     transcripts, but it is proven only on the path this board takes -
;     WL_ON SHORT-CIRCUITS when the pin is already an output and already
;     high, which on this board it always is. That short-circuit is the
;     fix for the original `WL_ON asserted (want 1) 0`: we had been
;     failing because we could not SET something that was already SET.
;     So the code that actually DRIVES the line from low to high has
;     still never been the thing that turned the radio on here.
;   * ANY SD CARD. This file drives the SDIO block at $FE300000, which
;     on a Pi 4 is the Wi-Fi chip. The card slot is EMMC2 at $FE340000
;     and belongs to emmc.pi4 - read its header, not this one.
;   * 4-BIT BUS WIDTH ABOVE THE RATES USED SO FAR, high-speed modes, and
;     1.8V signalling. Nothing here has been pushed past what the
;     firmware upload needed.
;
; Also: it compiles, and tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py runs the diagnostic
; against a modelled SDHCI controller whose expected register offsets
; and command encodings are parsed OUT OF THE VENDOR SOURCES rather than
; out of this file, so the gate and the library are two independent
; readings of one document and can disagree.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE CAPABILITIES REGISTER READS ZERO HERE AND THAT IS NOT A FAULT
; ======================================================================
; This file used to refuse with #SDIO_ERR_NO_CAPS - "Capabilities
; register all-zero or all-ones - wrong device?" - and that refusal cost
; the bench an afternoon looking at an address that was correct. The
; refusal is gone. Here is the argument for removing it, which is
; stronger than the argument that put it in.
;
; THE DRIVER RASPBERRY PI SHIPS FOR THIS EXACT BLOCK NEVER READS THAT
; REGISTER. mmcnr@7e300000's first compatible string is
; "brcm,bcm2835-mmc" and the driver behind it is on this disk:
; Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2835-mmc.c. In that whole file
; SDHCI_CAPABILITIES appears once, at lines 255-257, inside
; bcm2835_dumpregs()'s pr_debug block. It is printed and it is never
; used. Specifically:
;
;   * THE BASE CLOCK does not come from Capabilities bits 13:8. Line
;     1452: "host->max_clk = clk_get_rate(clk);" - the device tree's
;     clock, which for this node is BCM2835_CLOCK_EMMC (bcm270x.dtsi
;     line 42), which is firmware clock id 1. Line 1295 then derives
;     f_min from it. This file already had to ask the mailbox for that
;     number - see THE BASE CLOCK below - so it was never depending on
;     Capabilities for it either.
;
;   * THE 3.3 VOLT RAIL is not gated on SDHCI_CAN_VDD_330. Lines
;     1180-1182 write SDHCI_POWER_330 | SDHCI_POWER_ON unconditionally.
;
;   * THE HOST CAPABILITY FLAGS are hard-coded, lines 1302-1306:
;     MMC_CAP_CMD23, MMC_CAP_NEEDS_POLL, MMC_CAP_SDIO_IRQ, the two
;     HIGHSPEED bits and MMC_CAP2_SDIO_IRQ_NOTHREAD, all assigned, none
;     read from the controller.
;
; A driver that consults a register for nothing is a driver that has
; been written against silicon which does not populate it. An all-zero
; Capabilities on this block is therefore EXPECTED, not diagnostic, and
; a bring-up that stops there stops for no reason.
;
; WHAT REPLACES THE REFUSAL: the value is READ, RECORDED and REPORTED -
; SdioCaps0(), SdioCaps1() and SdioCapsAreZero() - and the sequence
; carries on. The remaining plausibility test is the Host Controller
; Version register, which on this board did answer, and which is the
; only register in the window with a value this file can argue about.
;
; #SDIO_ERR_NO_CAPS IS RETIRED, NOT RENUMBERED. Nothing raises it any
; more. Its number stays 4 and its slot stays occupied because the
; codes below it are printed into bench logs and referenced by
; tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py, and shifting thirty numbers to reclaim
; one would silently rewrite the meaning of every log already filed.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE CONTROLLER, AND WHY THE BRIEF'S "SDHOST" IS THE WRONG BLOCK
; ======================================================================
; This work was commissioned as "EMMC/SDHOST, mmcnr@7e300000". Those are
; two different controllers and only one of them is the right one. It is
; worth writing down which, because picking the wrong one produces the
; exact failure this project keeps paying for: stores to a real, powered,
; idle peripheral that produce no output and no error.
;
; THE BCM2711 HAS THREE SD CONTROLLERS:
;
;   sdhost   mmc@7e202000, "brcm,bcm2835-sdhost"
;            Datasheets/pi4/bcm283x.dtsi lines 314-320.
;            DISABLED on a Pi 4 Model B -
;            Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts lines 416-418:
;                &sdhost { status = "disabled"; };
;
;   sdhci    mmc@7e300000, "brcm,bcm2835-sdhci"
;            Datasheets/pi4/bcm283x.dtsi lines 423-429.
;            The Arasan block. THIS ONE.
;
;   emmc2    mmc@7e340000, "brcm,bcm2711-emmc2"
;            Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi lines 426-431.
;            The micro-SD socket. emmc.pi4 owns it.
;
; AND SDHOST CANNOT DO SDIO AT ALL. Not "is not used for"; cannot. From
; the header of the driver itself,
; Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_bcm2835_sdhost.c lines 5-13:
;
;     "The 2835 has two SD controllers: The Arasan sdhci controller
;      (supported by the iproc driver) and a custom sdhost controller
;      (supported by this driver).
;      The sdhci controller supports both sdcard and sdio.  The sdhost
;      controller supports the sdcard only, but has better performance.
;      Also note that the rpi3 has sdio wifi, so driving the sdcard with
;      the sdhost controller allows to use the sdhci controller for wifi
;      support."
;
; That file was fetched for this work and it is kept, because it is the
; citation for a NEGATIVE claim and negative claims are the ones that
; rot fastest. It is not the register map for anything here.
;
; WHAT mmcnr@7e300000 ACTUALLY IS: a second device-tree node over the
; SAME registers as sdhci, marked non-removable, so the downstream
; kernel can bind a different driver to it.
; Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm270x.dtsi lines 49-60:
;
;     /* A clone of mmc but with non-removable set */
;     mmcnr: mmcnr@7e300000 {
;         compatible = "brcm,bcm2835-mmc", "brcm,bcm2835-sdhci";
;         reg = <0x7e300000 0x100>;
;         ...
;         non-removable;
;
; and the WiFi part hangs off it -
; Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi lines 189-198:
;
;     &mmcnr {
;         ...
;         brcmf: wifi@1 {
;             reg = <1>;
;             compatible = "brcm,bcm4329-fmac";
;         };
;     };
;
; So: SDHCI registers, at bus $7E300000, window $100 bytes.
;
; THE BUS-TO-ARM TRANSLATION is the one already proven on this bench for
; two other peripherals. bcm2711.dtsi's soc node carries
; ranges = <0x7e000000 0x0 0xfe000000 0x01800000>, so bus $7Enn_nnnn is
; ARM $FEnn_nnnn in Low Peripheral mode; $300000 is inside the
; $01800000 window. uart.pi4 reaches the PL011 at $FE201000 and has
; PRINTED ON SILICON, and emmc.pi4 uses $FE340000 from the same
; translation. If the PL011 is where this map says it is, so is this.
;
; $FE300000 + $FF is the last byte this file may touch. The highest
; offset used is $FE, the Host Controller Version register.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE REGISTER MAP - WHERE IT CAME FROM, AND WHY IT IS BETTER SOURCED
; THAN emmc.pi4's
; ======================================================================
; emmc.pi4's header has to say, honestly, that its offsets come from the
; SD Host Controller Simplified Specification and that that document is
; not in this repository. This file does not have that problem, and the
; difference is worth a paragraph because it is the reason this file
; was allowed to exist at all under the cite-or-refuse rule.
;
; The node's compatible string is "brcm,bcm2835-mmc" first. That binds
; to a real driver whose source was fetched for this work:
;
;     Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2835-mmc.c
;     raspberrypi/linux rpi-6.12.y, drivers/mmc/host/bcm2835-mmc.c
;
; and that driver's register names come from
;
;     Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_sdhci.h
;     raspberrypi/linux rpi-6.12.y, drivers/mmc/host/sdhci.h
;
; EVERY offset and EVERY bit below carries a line number in that header.
; That is a file on this disk, not a specification somebody else has.
;
; TWO RULES THE BCM2711's ARASAN BLOCK IMPOSES, both read out of
; bcm2835-mmc.c, both of which will silently corrupt a register if
; ignored:
;
;   1. THIRTY-TWO BIT ACCESSES ONLY. bcm2835-mmc.c has no byte or
;      halfword store to this block at all: bcm2835_mmc_writeb (lines
;      178-186) and bcm2835_mmc_writew (lines 161-176) both READ the
;      enclosing 32-bit word, splice the field in, and write 32 bits
;      back. Everything here uses PeekN / PokeL, which are 32-bit, and
;      every 8- or 16-bit SDHCI register is reached by read-modify-write
;      of the word it lives in.
;
;   2. THE COMMAND REGISTER IS SHADOWED WITH TRANSFER MODE.
;      TRANSFER_MODE is 16 bits at $0C and COMMAND is 16 bits at $0E, so
;      they share one word. bcm2835_mmc_writew (line 162) keeps a
;      software shadow of TRANSFER_MODE and only commits it WHEN COMMAND
;      IS WRITTEN, because writing $0C on its own would also rewrite
;      $0E and re-issue the previous command. This file composes the
;      whole 32-bit word - transfer mode in the low half, command in the
;      high half - and writes it once, which is the same thing done
;      without the shadow, and is also exactly what emmc.pi4 does with
;      its CMDTM word.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PINS - GPIO 34..39, ALT3, AND THIS FILE MUXES THEM ITSELF
; ======================================================================
; Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi lines 317-321:
;
;     sdio_pins: sdio_pins {
;         brcm,pins =     <34 35 36 37 38 39>;
;         brcm,function = <BCM2835_FSEL_ALT3>; // alt3 = SD1
;         brcm,pull =     <0 2 2 2 2 2>;
;     };
;
; and Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts lines 271-276 attaches exactly
; that group to &mmcnr with bus-width = <4>.
;
; BCM2835_FSEL_ALT3 IS 7, not 3, and getting that wrong is a whole
; afternoon. Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_dt-bindings_pinctrl_bcm2835.h
; line 20:  #define BCM2835_FSEL_ALT3   7
; The device-tree names count in the order the FSEL field encodes, which
; is IN, OUT, ALT5, ALT4, ALT0, ALT1, ALT2, ALT3 - so ALT3 is the last
; encoding, 0b111.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE PULL ENCODING IS INVERTED BETWEEN THE DEVICE TREE AND THE BCM2711,
; AND THIS FILE HAD IT WRONG UNTIL 2026-08-26
; ======================================================================
; THE SENTENCE THAT USED TO BE HERE SAID THE OPPOSITE OF THE TRUTH. It
; read: "The same header gives the pulls: BCM2835_PUD_OFF 0,
; BCM2835_PUD_DOWN 1, BCM2835_PUD_UP 2 (lines 22-24), and those values
; are also what the BCM2711's GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL registers take." The
; last clause is false. It is preserved here, in full, because a
; correction that deletes the mistake teaches nobody why the mistake was
; easy.
;
; THE DEVICE TREE'S NUMBERING,
; Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_dt-bindings_pinctrl_bcm2835.h
; lines 22-24:
;
;     #define BCM2835_PUD_OFF     0
;     #define BCM2835_PUD_DOWN    1
;     #define BCM2835_PUD_UP      2
;
; THE HARDWARE'S NUMBERING, Datasheets/bcm2711-peripherals.txt Table 90
; at line 6304, the field description at lines 6315-6318:
;
;     00 = No resistor is selected
;     01 = Pull up resistor is selected
;     10 = Pull down resistor is selected
;     11 = Reserved
;
; UP AND DOWN ARE SWAPPED. The device tree says up is 2; the register
; says 2 is down. Both documents are correct about their own subject -
; the device tree value is consumed by a pinctrl driver that translates
; it, and this file has no pinctrl driver, so this file must write the
; register's numbering and not the device tree's.
;
; WHAT THAT DID. The source group says <0 2 2 2 2 2> and this file wrote
; those numbers straight through, so GPIO35..39 - CMD and DAT0..3 - were
; programmed b10, PULL DOWN, on lines that must idle HIGH. The board's
; answer to CMD5 was $00048000: SDHCI_INT_ERROR plus SDHCI_INT_END_BIT,
; a Command End Bit Error. The end bit of an SD response is the last bit
; before the card releases the line, and it must be 1; a pull-down
; dragging CMD towards 0 at exactly that moment is a textbook cause. The
; response arrived - that is what makes an end-bit error different from
; a timeout - and its final bit was wrong.
;
; THE CORROBORATION IS IN THIS TREE AND IT HAS PRINTED ON SILICON.
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4 lines 269-273 quotes Table 90 correctly and
; writes b01 for the pull-up on GPIO15. That file's output is how this
; bench reads every one of these logs. The precedent this file claimed
; to be following was right all along; only the transcription was wrong.
;
; SO THE PULLS ARE: GPIO34 (CLK) no resistor, GPIO35..39 (CMD, DAT0..3)
; PULL UP, written as #SDIO_PULL_UP, which is 1. That asymmetry is not
; an accident and it is not ours to tidy: a clock line is driven at both
; ends and wants no resistor, while a command or data line that nobody
; is driving must idle high or the controller reads a permanent start
; condition.
;
; AND THE ROLES ARE UNCHANGED, ONLY THE ENCODING. Do not "fix" this by
; changing which pins get pulled; the device tree's INTENT - none on the
; clock, up on the other five - was always right and is what is
; implemented. What changed is the number that means "up".
;
; WHY THIS FILE MUXES ITS OWN PINS instead of calling gpio.pi4's
; PinAlt() and PinPull(): the house rule is that a library never
; includes a library, and this is a bring-up dependency, not an optional
; extra - a driver that cannot reach its own pins is not a driver. The
; precedent is uart.pi4's uart_MuxPins(), which does the same thing for
; GPIO 14/15 for the same reason. sdio_MuxPins() below is that
; procedure's shape with different numbers.
;
; GPIO 34..39 ARE ALL IN GPFSEL3. GPFSEL registers hold ten pins each,
; three bits per pin, so pin p lives in GPFSEL(p/10) at bit (p%10)*3.
; 34..39 are pins 4..9 of GPFSEL3, bits 12 through 29. Bits 30 and 31 of
; that register are reserved and are left alone.
;
; THE PULL REGISTER IS THE BCM2711's, NOT THE BCM2835's. GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0
; is at GPIO base + $E4 and holds sixteen pins, two bits each - so 34..39
; are in REG2 at base + $EC, bits 4 through 15. There is NO GPPUD /
; GPPUDCLK clock-and-wait dance on this part; if you are porting Pi 3
; code and it has two delay loops in it, the delays are not the part to
; keep, the register is wrong. uart.pi4's header makes the same point at
; more length and it was learned there first.
;
; ======================================================================
; AN OPEN QUESTION: EVERY EXPANDER *GET* PASSES AND EVERY EXPANDER *SET*
; COMES BACK AS #MBX_ERR_TAG. TWO EXPLANATIONS FIT AND THEY ARE
; OPPOSITES. DO NOT ACT ON EITHER YET.
; ======================================================================
; THIS SECTION USED TO STATE A PLATFORM CONSTRAINT AND IT NO LONGER
; DOES. It said, in as many words, that this firmware lets a bare-metal
; program observe the expander and not command it. That conclusion was
; withdrawn on 2026-08-26 before it could be acted on, and the reason it
; is worth recording as a near-miss is that it was about to be written
; into this file as settled fact on the strength of one symptom.
;
; WHAT WAS MEASURED, and this part is not in doubt. Four expander
; property tags were sent to firmware GPIO 129 in one run:
;
;     GET_GPIO_CONFIG  $00030043   passed. direction 1, polarity 0.
;     GET_GPIO_STATE   $00030041   passed. level 1.
;     SET_GPIO_CONFIG  $00038043   came back as #MBX_ERR_TAG.
;     SET_GPIO_STATE   $00038041   came back as #MBX_ERR_TAG.
;
; EXPLANATION ONE - THE FIRMWARE DECLINES THE WRITES. #MBX_ERR_TAG is
; raised by mailbox.pi4 when the transaction completed, the whole-buffer
; code said SUCCESS, and that tag's own response bit was left clear.
; That is the property protocol's way of saying "received and not
; serviced". If it is the right reading, the expander is readable and
; not writable from here, and no amount of retrying would change it.
;
; EXPLANATION TWO - WE ARE MISREADING A SUCCESS. mailbox.pi4 checks
; EVERY INDIVIDUAL TAG's response bit and its own header records that
; this is STRICTER THAN U-BOOT, which checks only the whole-buffer code.
; A set-type tag has nothing it needs to return. If this firmware
; answers a SET with a zero-length response and the bit clear, our check
; would report #MBX_ERR_TAG on a tag the firmware actually honoured. On
; that reading all three sets worked and we disowned them. Another
; worker is testing this now; it is their question, not this file's.
;
; ---- WHAT THIS FILE'S OWN EVIDENCE SAYS ABOUT THE TWO ---------------
; Recorded here because it bears directly on somebody else's
; investigation, and stated as evidence rather than as a verdict.
;
;   1. A SET-TYPE TAG PASSED THE PER-TAG CHECK ON THIS FIRMWARE, IN THIS
;      RUN. SET_CLOCK_STATE $00038001 came back MBX_OK with a reply word
;      of 1. So the strict per-tag check does not reject set-type tags
;      as a class, and whatever is happening to the two GPIO sets is not
;      simply "mailbox.pi4 is strict about SETs". That is the strongest
;      single fact either way and it argues for explanation one.
;
;   2. ALL THREE SETS ARE DOCUMENTED TO RETURN A PAYLOAD, so none of
;      them is the zero-length case in the abstract. The expander
;      driver reads set_out.gpio back and tests it - lines 124-127 for
;      the config path, lines 191-194 for the state path - which it
;      could not do if the firmware returned nothing. That is what the
;      KERNEL expects, though, not what this firmware was seen to do.
;
;   3. WL_ON READING BACK HIGH IS NOT EVIDENCE FOR EITHER. It was
;      already high before this file wrote anything: the level came from
;      GET_GPIO_STATE at rung 6, ahead of both sets. The firmware had
;      powered the radio at boot, which is consistent with
;      bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts lines 268-270 deleting the wifi-pwrseq node.
;      A successful write and a no-op write are indistinguishable on a
;      pin that was already in the wanted state, so this observation
;      discriminates nothing.
;
; ---- HOW TO SETTLE IT, CHEAPLY --------------------------------------
; SdioWlOnConfigRespBytes() and SdioClockSetRespBytes() report
; MailboxRespBytes() captured straight after each set - the length field
; of that tag's own code word. A zero length beside a cleared response
; bit is explanation two's signature; a set-type tag that returns its
; documented eight bytes while still failing the check is not.
;
; ---- AND SO NOTHING HERE DEPENDS ON THE ANSWER ----------------------
; SdioAssertWlOn() reads first and writes only if it must, and on this
; board it sends no write at all. That is correct under BOTH readings,
; which is why it was safe to land while the question is open. If
; explanation two turns out to be right, nothing in this file has to
; change; only this section does.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE POWER RAIL - WL_ON, AND THIS FILE NOW ASSERTS IT
; ======================================================================
; The CYW43455's enable pin is not on any GPIO this file can reach. It
; is on the firmware-owned GPIO expander:
; Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts lines 61-70,
;
;     &expgpio {
;         gpio-line-names = "BT_ON",        /*  0 */
;                           "WL_ON",
;                           ...
;
; and that expander is only reachable through the VideoCore property
; mailbox. Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_gpio-raspberrypi-exp.c
; line 20 gives the offset that turns an expander pin into a firmware
; GPIO number:
;
;     #define RPI_EXP_GPIO_BASE   128
;
; used at line 111 as "set_out.gpio = off + RPI_EXP_GPIO_BASE". So
; WL_ON is firmware GPIO 129, and driving it high is
; RPI_FIRMWARE_SET_GPIO_CONFIG ($00038043,
; Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h line 88) with the six-word
; payload from that file's struct gpio_set_config (lines 32-39):
;
;     gpio, direction, polarity, term_en, term_pull_up, state
;
; with direction = OUT and state = 1. Thereafter
; RPI_FIRMWARE_SET_GPIO_STATE ($00038041, header line 85) with
; { gpio, state } is enough to toggle it (struct gpio_get_set_state,
; lines 49-52 of the expander driver).
;
; THE 128 IS CORROBORATED BY A SECOND, INDEPENDENT SOURCE, and it is
; worth writing down because the whole radio hangs off that one number.
; Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md documents
; tags $00030041 and $00038041 under their ORIGINAL name, "Get/Set
; onboard LED status", and lists the legal pin numbers at lines 322-324:
; "42: status led" and "130: power led". Expander line 2 is named
; "PWR_LED_OFF" in bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts line 64. 128 + 2 = 130. Two
; documents written years apart, for two different purposes, agree on
; the base. The tag was later generalised from LEDs to the whole
; expander, which is why the modern kernel header calls the same numbers
; GET_GPIO_STATE and SET_GPIO_STATE.
;
; THE PAYLOAD SIZES ARE NOT ALL THE SAME AND GETTING ONE WRONG IS A
; SILENT REFUSAL. rpi_firmware_property() sends ONE size that serves as
; both the request length and the response buffer length -
; Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.c line 172,
; "header->buf_size = buf_size" - and that size is sizeof() of the
; struct the caller passed. The three structs, from the expander driver:
;
;     struct gpio_set_config      lines 32-39   6 words   24 bytes
;     struct gpio_get_config      lines 41-47   5 words   20 bytes
;     struct gpio_get_set_state   lines 49-52   2 words    8 bytes
;
; GET_GPIO_CONFIG IS TWENTY BYTES, NOT TWENTY-FOUR. The set struct has a
; trailing "state" field and the get struct does not. The diagnostic
; asked for 24 on its first ever run and WL_ON came back 0; that is the
; leading suspect and it is fixed in both files.
;
; WHY THIS FILE DOES IT NOW. The old text here said sdio.pi4 could not,
; because it would then only compile beside mailbox.pi4. That trade was
; taken and it was the wrong one - see MAILBOX.PI4 IS NOW A HARD
; REQUIREMENT above. The sequence is: read the polarity back with
; GET_GPIO_CONFIG and keep it (the expander driver does exactly this at
; lines 117-120, "Retain existing setting" - inventing a polarity is a
; one-in-two chance of switching the radio OFF while reporting success),
; then SET_GPIO_CONFIG with direction OUT and state 1, then
; SET_GPIO_STATE with state 1 as well. The last of those three is
; belt-and-braces and costs one transaction: the config call is what
; rpi_exp_gpio_dir_out() does and the state call is what
; rpi_exp_gpio_set() does, and a driver that has never seen this
; firmware answer should do both rather than assume the first implies
; the second.
;
; AND WL_ON IS NOT ASSERTED FOR US AT BOOT. bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts lines
; 268-270 delete the wifi-pwrseq node outright, so Linux does not
; sequence it on this board either; the firmware owns it. The board has
; now said what that means in practice for a bare-metal image, and the
; answer was "WL_ON asserted (want 1) 0" - the very first thing the
; diagnostic printed. Whatever start4.elf leaves behind, it is not a
; radio this file can assume is awake.
;
; ======================================================================
; IS THE CONTROLLER ITSELF POWER-GATED OR CLOCK-GATED? - WHAT THE
; SOURCES ON THIS DISK DO AND DO NOT SETTLE
; ======================================================================
; The question that produced this section: the Host Controller Version
; register answered and Capabilities read zero, so is the block being
; held unpowered or unclocked? The answer turned out to be that the
; premise was wrong - see THE CAPABILITIES REGISTER READS ZERO above -
; but the power and clock questions were worth asking and the answers
; are worth keeping, because the next person will ask them too.
;
; THERE IS NO SD OR SDIO POWER DOMAIN. The newer firmware interface,
; SET_DOMAIN_STATE $00038030 / GET_DOMAIN_STATE $00030030
; (Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h lines 77 and
; 86), takes a domain index from
; Datasheets/pi4/v3d/linux/rpi-power-bindings.h lines 12-36. That list
; is twenty-three entries long - I2C0, I2C1, I2C2, VIDEO_SCALER, VPU1,
; HDMI, USB, VEC, JPEG, H264, V3D, ISP, UNICAM0, UNICAM1, CCP2RX, CSI2,
; CPI, DSI0, DSI1, TRANSPOSER, CCP2TX, CDP, ARM - and there is no SD, no
; SDIO, no EMMC and no MMC anywhere in it. So the domain interface is
; not the route to this controller and this file does not send that tag.
;
; THE OLDER INTERFACE HAS A DEVICE ID THAT MIGHT BE THIS BLOCK, AND
; NOTHING ON THIS DISK SAYS WHETHER IT IS. SET_POWER_STATE $00028001
; and GET_POWER_STATE $00020001 take a device id, and id 0 is named two
; different things by the two sources here:
;
;     Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h line 177
;         #define BCM2835_MBOX_POWER_DEVID_SDHCI   0
;     Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md line 168
;         * 0x00000000: SD Card
;
; "SDHCI" would be the Arasan block this file drives. "SD Card" would be
; the micro-SD socket, which on a BCM2711 is EMMC2 and is emmc.pi4's.
; Those are different controllers and the two documents do not agree
; about which one id 0 means. The same doc then lists ids 9 and 10 as
; "Unknown (RPi4)" (lines 177-178), which is a written admission that
; the device-id table was never updated for this part.
;
; SO THIS FILE ASKS RATHER THAN ASSERTS. sdio_QueryPower() sends
; GET_POWER_STATE for one device id and records the two answer bits -
; bit 0 on/off, bit 1 "device does not exist"
; (firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md lines 190-193). SdioInit()
; queries id 0, and the diagnostic sweeps 0 through 10 and prints the
; table, because one bench run settles what no file here does.
;
; THE ONLY THING THIS FILE WILL SET is device id 0 to ON, and only when
; the firmware has just said that id 0 exists and is OFF. Turning
; something on that is already on is a no-op; turning the SD card's
; controller on when it is already running the boot medium is the same
; no-op. Nothing here ever sends state 0, to any device id, ever. A
; driver for the radio has no business switching the boot medium off,
; and if id 0 does mean "SD Card" that is exactly what an "off" would
; do.
;
; THE CLOCK. mmcnr@7e300000 declares BCM2835_CLOCK_EMMC
; (Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm270x.dtsi line 42) and the
; mailbox id for that clock is 1
; (Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_bcm283x_mbox.h line 229). See THE BASE
; CLOCK below for why it is 1 and not 12.
;
; AND THE 250 MHz THE FIRST BENCH RUN PRINTED DOES NOT MEAN THE CLOCK IS
; RUNNING. GET_CLOCK_RATE $00030002 is documented at
; firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md line 310 as: "Next enable rate
; should be returned even if the clock is not running." It is the rate
; the clock WOULD have. A gated clock answers that question with a
; number and tells you nothing. The tag that answers the real question
; is GET_CLOCK_STATE $00030001, whose response bit 0 is 0=off / 1=on and
; whose bit 1 is 0=exists / 1=does not exist (same document, lines
; 257-271); SET_CLOCK_STATE $00038001 takes the same two words back
; (lines 273-292). Both numbers are also in
; Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h at lines 55 and
; 81. A second, independent witness costs one more transaction:
; GET_CLOCK_MEASURED $00030047 reports what the firmware OBSERVES rather
; than what it intends, and mailbox.pi4 already wraps it as
; MailboxClockRateMeasured(). A configured 250 MHz beside a measured 0
; would be a gated clock saying so out loud.
;
; THIS FILE TURNS CLOCK 1 ON IF, AND ONLY IF, THE FIRMWARE SAYS IT
; EXISTS AND IS OFF. It never turns a clock off.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE BASE CLOCK - THE WEAKEST NUMBER HERE, AND IT REFUSES
; ======================================================================
; SDHCI puts the base clock in the Capabilities register:
; rpi-6.12.y_sdhci.h lines 214-215, SDHCI_CLOCK_BASE_MASK is bits 13:8
; in MHz. A zero there means "not obtainable from this register", which
; is what Broadcom's blocks report, and it is why U-Boot's driver for
; the neighbouring controller asks the firmware instead:
; v2025.01_bcm2835_sdhost.c line 784,
;
;     host->max_clk = bcm2835_get_mmc_clock(BCM2835_MBOX_CLOCK_ID_CORE);
;
; THE CLOCK ID FOR THIS CONTROLLER IS *EMMC*, NOT EMMC2 AND NOT CORE.
; bcm270x.dtsi line 42 gives mmcnr clocks = <&clocks BCM2835_CLOCK_EMMC>,
; and the mailbox id for that is 1 -
; Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_bcm283x_mbox.h line 229:
;
;     #define BCM2835_MBOX_CLOCK_ID_EMMC   1
;
; while EMMC2, the micro-SD controller emmc.pi4 drives, is 12 (line
; 239). THOSE ARE DIFFERENT CLOCKS AND THEY DO NOT HAVE TO BE THE SAME
; RATE. The 250000 kHz that fat.pi4's header records as measured for
; EMMC2 is EMMC2's number. Do not paste it in here; ask the mailbox for
; clock 1 and pass what it says.
;
; SO: if Capabilities reports zero and nobody has called
; SdioSetBaseClockKhz(), SdioInit() REFUSES with #SDIO_ERR_BASECLK
; rather than inventing a divisor. A wrong base clock does not fail
; loudly - it produces a bus running at some other speed, which works
; for CMD0 and fails somewhere much later and much less obviously.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
; ======================================================================
;   NO DMA, NO SDMA, NO ADMA. Polled PIO through the Buffer Data Port,
;   like emmc.pi4. The transfers this bus needs for chip identification
;   are four bytes at a time; a scatter-gather engine for that would be
;   ceremony.
;
;   NO CARD INTERRUPT. SDIO's asynchronous interrupt (SDHCI_INT_CARD_INT,
;   sdhci.h line 145) is how a radio says "I have a packet". Nothing here
;   fields it, because nothing here has anything to receive - see
;   cyw43.pi4 and the firmware wall. The bit is left MASKED, deliberately,
;   so that a stuck level-triggered card interrupt cannot wedge the
;   polling loops.
;
;   NO HIGH SPEED, NO UHS, NO 1.8V SWITCH. The bus runs at Default Speed.
;   Signal-voltage switching needs a regulator this file cannot reach and
;   a tuning sequence it cannot verify.
;
;   NO ODD TRANSFER SIZES ON CMD53. Whole 4-byte units only, in both
;   byte mode and block mode - see the CMD53 section for why the Buffer
;   Data Port's width makes that a real limit rather than a lazy one.
;
;   BLOCK MODE ON CMD53 EXISTS AS OF 2026-08-26 and this entry used to
;   say it did not. The old text - "byte mode only ... that is enough
;   for every register access above it" - was true while the only
;   caller was a chip-identification path moving four bytes at a time.
;   It stopped being true the moment a 609 KB firmware image landed on
;   the disk, because 609 KB in 512-byte byte-mode commands is over a
;   thousand commands, and a 1520-byte WLAN frame cannot be expressed in
;   byte mode at all. SdioCmd53ReadBlocks / SdioCmd53WriteBlocks are the
;   entry points; the byte-mode pair still takes the same path it was
;   proven on.
;
;   NO CIS PARSING. The card's tuple chain is not read. Nothing in this
;   project needs the manufacturer ID that badly, and a parser for a
;   structure nobody can test is a liability.
;
; ======================================================================
; CITATIONS - THE FULL LIST
; ======================================================================
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_sdhci.h
;       every register offset and controller bit. Line numbers inline.
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2835-mmc.c
;       32-bit-access rule, the COMMAND/TRANSFER_MODE shadow, the
;       transfer-mode composition, and the NEGATIVE claim that carries
;       the most weight in this file: Capabilities is read only to be
;       printed (255-257), max_clk comes from the clock (1452), the
;       3.3V rail is written unconditionally (1180-1182) and the host
;       capability flags are hard-coded (1302-1306).
;   Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md
;       the power device-id list and its two "Unknown (RPi4)" entries,
;       the GET/SET_POWER_STATE and GET/SET_CLOCK_STATE payloads and
;       state bits, "next enable rate ... even if the clock is not
;       running", and the LED pin numbers that corroborate the
;       expander's base of 128.
;   Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h
;       the full tag enum: GET/SET_POWER_STATE, GET/SET_CLOCK_STATE,
;       GET/SET_DOMAIN_STATE, GET/SET_GPIO_STATE, GET/SET_GPIO_CONFIG.
;   Datasheets/pi4/v3d/linux/rpi-power-bindings.h
;       the twenty-three power domains, none of which is an SD or SDIO
;       one. Filed under v3d because that is what it was fetched for.
;   Datasheets/pi4/v3d/linux/raspberrypi-power.c
;       how a domain is asked for, and that the old SET_POWER_STATE
;       interface and the new SET_DOMAIN_STATE interface are two
;       different numbering schemes over one transport.
;   Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h
;       BCM2835_MBOX_POWER_DEVID_SDHCI = 0, and the set/get power tag
;       structures.
;   Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.c
;       one buf_size serves as both request and response length.
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm270x.dtsi
;       mmcnr@7e300000, its compatible strings and its clock.
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi
;       sdio_pins, and the wifi@1 child node.
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_dt-bindings_pinctrl_bcm2835.h
;       BCM2835_FSEL_ALT3 = 7, the pull encodings.
;   Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_gpio-raspberrypi-exp.c
;       RPI_EXP_GPIO_BASE = 128, the SET_GPIO_CONFIG payload.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h
;       CMD5/CMD52/CMD53 opcodes, the argument layouts, R4 and R5, CCCR.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_ops.c
;       the exact CMD52 and CMD53 argument packing, and sdio_reset.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_io.c
;       sdio_enable_func - IOEx then poll IORx.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_core_sdio.c
;       the attach order, the 4-bit switch, functions-from-R4.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_mmc.h
;       CMD0, CMD7, MMC_CARD_BUSY.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_sd.h
;       CMD3.
;   Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_linux_mmc_sd_ops.c
;       RCA is resp[0] >> 16.
;   Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_bcm2835_sdhost.c
;       the NEGATIVE claim: sdhost cannot do SDIO.
;   Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_bcm283x_mbox.h
;       BCM2835_MBOX_CLOCK_ID_EMMC = 1.
;   Datasheets/pi4/bcm283x.dtsi, bcm2711.dtsi, bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts
;       the three controllers, and sdhost being disabled on this board.
;   Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h
;       the two GPIO property tags.
;   CNTPCT_EL0 / CNTFRQ_EL0
;       ARMv8-A architectural registers. Not Broadcom's, not in any
;       Broadcom document. The form is anvil.pi4's, by way of
;       emmc.pi4 and safety.pi4.
;
;   NOT CITED AND THEREFORE NOT PRESENT: any 1.8V signalling sequence,
;   any tuning sequence, any DMA descriptor format, the CIS tuple
;   format, and every CCCR register above $17.
; ======================================================================


; ======================================================================
;  THE CONTROLLER
; ======================================================================

; mmcnr@7e300000 -> ARM $FE300000. See THE CONTROLLER above.
#SDIO_BASE = $FE300000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SDHCI register offsets. Every one from rpi-6.12.y_sdhci.h, line given.
;
;  The 8- and 16-bit registers are named at their SPEC offsets here even
;  though nothing accesses them at those widths - see rule 1 in the
;  header. The word they live in is (offset & ~3).
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_ARG2        = $00   ; SDHCI_DMA_ADDRESS / ARGUMENT2      h:26
#SDIO_BLKSIZECNT  = $04   ; BLOCK_SIZE(16)@$04 + BLOCK_COUNT(16)@$06
                          ;                                    h:30, h:33
#SDIO_ARG1        = $08   ; SDHCI_ARGUMENT                     h:35
#SDIO_CMDTM       = $0C   ; TRANSFER_MODE(16)@$0C + COMMAND(16)@$0E
                          ;                                    h:37, h:45
#SDIO_RESP0       = $10   ; SDHCI_RESPONSE                     h:61
#SDIO_RESP1       = $14
#SDIO_RESP2       = $18
#SDIO_RESP3       = $1C
#SDIO_BUFFER      = $20   ; SDHCI_BUFFER                       h:63
#SDIO_STATUS      = $24   ; SDHCI_PRESENT_STATE                h:65
#SDIO_CONTROL0    = $28   ; HOST_CONTROL(8)@$28 + POWER_CONTROL(8)@$29
                          ;                                    h:82, h:98
#SDIO_CONTROL1    = $2C   ; CLOCK_CONTROL(16)@$2C + TIMEOUT(8)@$2E
                          ;             + SOFTWARE_RESET(8)@$2F
                          ;                            h:118, h:128, h:130
#SDIO_INT_STATUS  = $30   ; SDHCI_INT_STATUS                   h:135
#SDIO_INT_ENABLE  = $34   ; SDHCI_INT_ENABLE                   h:136
#SDIO_SIG_ENABLE  = $38   ; SDHCI_SIGNAL_ENABLE                h:137
#SDIO_CAPS0       = $40   ; SDHCI_CAPABILITIES                 h:210
#SDIO_CAPS1       = $44   ; SDHCI_CAPABILITIES_1               h:231
#SDIO_HOST_VER    = $FC   ; SDHCI_HOST_VERSION is $FE          h:279
                          ; - the 16-bit register in the TOP half of the
                          ; word at $FC, which is what gets read.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Present State, sdhci.h lines 66-72.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_ST_CMD_INHIBIT = $00000001   ; SDHCI_CMD_INHIBIT        h:66
#SDIO_ST_DAT_INHIBIT = $00000002   ; SDHCI_DATA_INHIBIT       h:67
#SDIO_ST_SPACE_AVAIL = $00000400   ; SDHCI_SPACE_AVAILABLE    h:70
#SDIO_ST_DATA_AVAIL  = $00000800   ; SDHCI_DATA_AVAILABLE     h:71

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Host Control and Power Control, both bytes of the word at $28.
;  Host Control is byte 0, Power Control is byte 1, so a Power Control
;  value goes in at bits 15:8.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_HC_4BITBUS  = $00000002      ; SDHCI_CTRL_4BITBUS       h:85
#SDIO_HC_HISPD    = $00000004      ; SDHCI_CTRL_HISPD         h:86  unused
#SDIO_PWR_ON      = $00000100      ; SDHCI_POWER_ON      $01  h:99
#SDIO_PWR_330     = $00000E00      ; SDHCI_POWER_330     $0E  h:102
#SDIO_PWR_MASK    = $0000FF00

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Clock Control / Timeout Control / Software Reset - the word at $2C.
;  Clock Control is bits 15:0, Timeout Control bits 23:16, Software
;  Reset bits 31:24.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_CLK_INT_EN     = $00000001   ; SDHCI_CLOCK_INT_EN       h:127
#SDIO_CLK_INT_STABLE = $00000002   ; SDHCI_CLOCK_INT_STABLE   h:126
#SDIO_CLK_CARD_EN    = $00000004   ; SDHCI_CLOCK_CARD_EN      h:125
; SDHCI_DIVIDER_SHIFT 8 (h:119), SDHCI_DIVIDER_HI_SHIFT 6 (h:120),
; SDHCI_DIV_MASK $FF (h:121), SDHCI_DIV_HI_MASK $300 (h:123). The low
; eight divisor bits sit at word bits 15:8 and the high two at 7:6.
#SDIO_TIMEOUT_MAX    = $000E0000   ; Timeout Control = $E, the longest
                                   ; the field encodes. The register is
                                   ; named at sdhci.h:128; the value is a
                                   ; choice, and it is the same choice
                                   ; emmc.pi4 makes for the same reason -
                                   ; a short data timeout on a bus that
                                   ; has never run is a self-inflicted
                                   ; wound.
#SDIO_SRST_ALL       = $01000000   ; SDHCI_RESET_ALL          h:131
#SDIO_SRST_CMD       = $02000000   ; SDHCI_RESET_CMD          h:132
#SDIO_SRST_DATA      = $04000000   ; SDHCI_RESET_DATA         h:133

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Interrupt Status bits, sdhci.h lines 138-158.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_INT_RESPONSE    = $00000001  ; SDHCI_INT_RESPONSE       h:138
#SDIO_INT_DATA_END    = $00000002  ; SDHCI_INT_DATA_END       h:139
#SDIO_INT_SPACE_AVAIL = $00000010  ; SDHCI_INT_SPACE_AVAIL    h:142
#SDIO_INT_DATA_AVAIL  = $00000020  ; SDHCI_INT_DATA_AVAIL     h:143
#SDIO_INT_CARD_INT    = $00000100  ; SDHCI_INT_CARD_INT       h:146
#SDIO_INT_ERROR       = $00008000  ; SDHCI_INT_ERROR          h:150
#SDIO_INT_TIMEOUT     = $00010000  ; SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT        h:151
#SDIO_INT_CRC         = $00020000  ; SDHCI_INT_CRC            h:152
#SDIO_INT_END_BIT     = $00040000  ; SDHCI_INT_END_BIT        h:153
#SDIO_INT_INDEX       = $00080000  ; SDHCI_INT_INDEX          h:154
#SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK  = $FFFF8000  ; SDHCI_INT_ERROR_MASK     h:166
#SDIO_INT_ALL         = $FFFFFFFF  ; SDHCI_INT_ALL_MASK       h:172

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE FOUR COMMAND-ERROR BITS ARE NOT THE FOUR DATA-ERROR BITS, AND
;  LINUX'S NAMES DO NOT SAY SO. sdhci.h calls the command ones
;  SDHCI_INT_TIMEOUT / _CRC / _END_BIT / _INDEX with no "CMD" in the
;  name (lines 151-154), and the data ones SDHCI_INT_DATA_TIMEOUT /
;  _DATA_CRC / _DATA_END_BIT (lines 155-157). Reading a status word by
;  eye and landing on the wrong group of four is easy, which is why
;  SdioIntBitName() below exists and why the diagnostic prints names
;  instead of a hexadecimal word.
;
;  $00048000, which is what this board answered CMD5 with, is
;  SDHCI_INT_ERROR (bit 15, the summary) plus SDHCI_INT_END_BIT (bit
;  18) - a COMMAND End Bit Error. Not a data one, and not a timeout.
;  A timeout would have meant nothing on the bus answered at all; an
;  end-bit error means a response arrived and its last bit was not 1.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; Enable every status bit EXCEPT the card interrupt. See "NO CARD
; INTERRUPT" in the header - a level-triggered SDIO interrupt that
; nobody services stays asserted for ever, and the poll loops below all
; test the whole status word.
#SDIO_INT_ENABLE_SET  = $FFFFFEFF

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The 32-bit CMDTM word: transfer mode in the low half, command in the
;  high half.
;
;  sdhci.h line 57 defines the 16-bit command register as
;      SDHCI_MAKE_CMD(c, f) = ((c & 0xff) << 8) | (f & 0xff)
;  and that register is bits 31:16 of this word, so the command INDEX
;  lands at bits 31:24 and the command FLAGS at bits 23:16.
;
;  The flags, from sdhci.h lines 47-55, shifted left 16:
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_CMD_RSP_NONE  = $00000000    ; SDHCI_CMD_RESP_NONE  $00 h:52
#SDIO_CMD_RSP_136   = $00010000    ; SDHCI_CMD_RESP_LONG  $01 h:53
#SDIO_CMD_RSP_48    = $00020000    ; SDHCI_CMD_RESP_SHORT $02 h:54
#SDIO_CMD_RSP_48B   = $00030000    ; ..._SHORT_BUSY       $03 h:55
#SDIO_CMD_RSP_MASK  = $00030000    ; SDHCI_CMD_RESP_MASK  $03 h:46
#SDIO_CMD_CRCCHK    = $00080000    ; SDHCI_CMD_CRC        $08 h:47
#SDIO_CMD_IXCHK     = $00100000    ; SDHCI_CMD_INDEX      $10 h:48
#SDIO_CMD_ISDATA    = $00200000    ; SDHCI_CMD_DATA       $20 h:49
#SDIO_CMD_INDEX_STEP = $01000000   ; one command index

; Transfer mode, sdhci.h lines 38-44, in the low half of the word.
#SDIO_TM_BLKCNT_EN  = $00000002    ; SDHCI_TRNS_BLK_CNT_EN    h:39
#SDIO_TM_READ       = $00000010    ; SDHCI_TRNS_READ          h:42
; MULTI is set whenever the transfer is more than ONE block, and the
; rule is bcm2835-mmc.c line 632's, not a guess:
;     if ((mmc_op_multi(cmd->opcode) || data->blocks > 1))
;         mode |= SDHCI_TRNS_MULTI;
; mmc_op_multi() is false for CMD53 - it names CMD18, CMD25, CMD12 and
; CMD23 only - so for this file the whole condition reduces to
; "blocks > 1", which is exactly how sdio_Cmd53Xfer applies it.
#SDIO_TM_MULTI      = $00000020    ; SDHCI_TRNS_MULTI         h:44

; AUTO CMD12 IS DELIBERATELY NEVER SET, and this is worth stating because
; a multi-block transfer on an SD MEMORY card would need it. CMD53 is not
; an open-ended memory read: the block count in the argument tells the
; card exactly how many blocks are coming and it stops on its own. There
; is no CMD12 in the SDIO protocol at all - the abort mechanism is a
; CMD52 write to CCCR $06 (#SDIO_CCCR_ABORT), which SdioInit already
; issues as its card-reset step at line 3180 below. In
; bcm2835-mmc.c the flag guarding it (SDHCI_AUTO_CMD12, line 125) is
; declared and TESTED at line 639 but never SET anywhere in the file, so
; even for memory that driver does not use it.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE COMMANDS. Index * $01000000, then the flags.
;
;  Which flags each command gets is not a free choice: CRC check and
;  index check may only be enabled for responses that CARRY a CRC and an
;  index. R1, R5, R6 and R7 do; R2 carries a CRC but no index; R3 and R4
;  carry neither, and turning the checks on for them makes the controller
;  reject a perfectly good response. That rule is why CMD5 below looks
;  bare next to CMD3.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; CMD0 GO_IDLE_STATE. No response at all.
;   MMC_GO_IDLE_STATE 0 - v6.12_linux_mmc_mmc.h line 31.
#SDIO_CMD0  = $00000000

; CMD3 SEND_RELATIVE_ADDR, R6. Checked both ways.
;   SD_SEND_RELATIVE_ADDR 3 - v6.12_linux_mmc_sd.h line 14.
#SDIO_CMD3  = $031A0000

; CMD5 IO_SEND_OP_COND, R4. NO CRC, NO index check - R4 has neither.
;   SD_IO_SEND_OP_COND 5 - v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h line 12.
#SDIO_CMD5  = $05020000

; CMD7 SELECT_CARD, R1b. The busy period is on DAT0 after the response.
;   MMC_SELECT_CARD 7 - v6.12_linux_mmc_mmc.h line 38.
#SDIO_CMD7  = $071B0000

; CMD52 IO_RW_DIRECT, R5. Index $34.
;   SD_IO_RW_DIRECT 52 - v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h line 13.
#SDIO_CMD52 = $341A0000

; CMD53 IO_RW_EXTENDED, R5, with data. Index $35. The low half carries
; the transfer mode: block-count enable always (bcm2835-mmc.c line 630
; sets SDHCI_TRNS_BLK_CNT_EN unconditionally whenever there is data),
; plus the read bit for a read.
;   SD_IO_RW_EXTENDED 53 - v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h line 14.
#SDIO_CMD53_READ  = $353A0012
#SDIO_CMD53_WRITE = $353A0002

; The same two commands for a transfer of MORE THAN ONE BLOCK: identical
; except for SDHCI_TRNS_MULTI ($20) in the transfer-mode half. Written
; out as whole constants rather than composed with an Or at the call
; site, for the same reason the two single-block forms are: the transfer
; mode and the command index must reach the controller as ONE 32-bit
; store, and a value assembled in four places is a value that can differ
; in one of them.
#SDIO_CMD53_READ_MULTI  = $353A0032
#SDIO_CMD53_WRITE_MULTI = $353A0022

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CMD52 / CMD53 argument layout.
;
;  v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_ops.c lines 75-80, mmc_io_rw_direct_host:
;      cmd.arg  = write ? 0x80000000 : 0x00000000;
;      cmd.arg |= fn << 28;
;      cmd.arg |= (write && out) ? 0x08000000 : 0;      <- the RAW flag
;      cmd.arg |= addr << 9;
;      cmd.arg |= in;
;  and line 72 bounds the address: "if (addr & ~0x1FFFF) return -EINVAL".
;
;  v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_ops.c lines 136-143, mmc_io_rw_extended:
;      cmd.arg  = write ? 0x80000000 : 0x00000000;
;      cmd.arg |= fn << 28;
;      cmd.arg |= incr_addr ? 0x04000000 : 0;
;      cmd.arg |= addr << 9;
;      if (blocks == 0) cmd.arg |= (blksz == 512) ? 0 : blksz;  byte mode
;      else             cmd.arg |= 0x08000000 | blocks;         block mode
;
;  NOTE THE COLLISION AT BIT 27 and do not let it bite: in CMD52 it is
;  the RAW (read-after-write) flag, in CMD53 it is BLOCK MODE. Same bit,
;  two unrelated meanings, and the two constants are named apart here so
;  a copy-paste between the two builders cannot go quiet.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_ARG_WRITE     = $80000000
#SDIO_ARG_FN_STEP   = $10000000    ; fn << 28
#SDIO_ARG_RAW       = $08000000    ; CMD52 only
#SDIO_ARG_BLOCKMODE = $08000000    ; CMD53 only - NOT the same thing
#SDIO_ARG_INCR      = $04000000    ; CMD53 only
#SDIO_ARG_ADDR_STEP = $00000200    ; addr << 9
#SDIO_ADDR_MAX      = $0001FFFF    ; sdio_ops.c line 72

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TWO LENGTH FIELDS ARE BOTH NINE BITS AND THEY DO NOT MEAN THE
;  SAME THING, which is the whole reason block mode exists here.
;
;  In BYTE mode the nine bits are a BYTE count, so one command moves at
;  most 512 bytes and 512 is spelled 0.
;
;  In BLOCK mode the nine bits are a BLOCK count, so one command moves
;  at most (blocks * block size) bytes. With F1's 64-byte block that is
;  32,704 bytes per command; with F2's 512-byte block it is 261,632.
;  Zero here means "keep going until an abort", which this file does not
;  implement and therefore refuses - an infinite transfer that nothing
;  ever stops is a hung bus, not a fast one.
;
;  511 rather than 512: 512 does not fit in nine bits either way, and
;  unlike the byte-mode case there is no "0 means 512" convention to
;  fall back on, because 0 is already taken by the infinite form.
;  sdio_ops.c line 142 is the block-mode line and it has no special case
;  at all:
;      cmd.arg |= 0x08000000 | blocks;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_BLOCKS_MAX    = 511
; The SDHCI BLOCK_COUNT register is sixteen bits wide (h:33) and
; bcm2835-mmc.c line 583 says so too - "BUG_ON(data->blocks > 65535)".
; The 511 above is therefore the CARD's limit, not the host's, and it is
; the tighter of the two. Both are recorded because a future reader
; comparing this file with that one should not have to work out which
; number bit.
#SDIO_HOST_BLOCKS_MAX = 65535

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  R5, the CMD52/CMD53 response. v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h lines 55-61.
;  Bits 7:0 are the data byte; 15:8 are the status flags.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_R5_CRC_ERROR   = $00008000   ; R5_COM_CRC_ERROR         s:55
#SDIO_R5_ILLEGAL_CMD = $00004000   ; R5_ILLEGAL_COMMAND       s:56
#SDIO_R5_ERROR       = $00000800   ; R5_ERROR                 s:57
#SDIO_R5_FUNC_NUMBER = $00000200   ; R5_FUNCTION_NUMBER       s:58
#SDIO_R5_OUT_OF_RANGE = $00000100  ; R5_OUT_OF_RANGE          s:59
; R5_STATUS(x) is (x & 0xCB00) - line 60. Every one of those five bits
; means the card refused, so they are tested as one mask.
#SDIO_R5_BAD         = $0000CB00

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  R4, the CMD5 response. v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h lines 37-38 and
;  v6.12_linux_mmc_core_sdio.c line 1269.
;
;      [31]    busy / initialisation complete   (MMC_CARD_BUSY,
;              v6.12_linux_mmc_mmc.h line 206 - and note the name is
;              backwards: SET means DONE, not busy)
;      [30:28] number of I/O functions          (ocr & 0x70000000) >> 28
;      [27]    memory present                   R4_MEMORY_PRESENT
;      [24]    1.8V present                     R4_18V_PRESENT
;      [23:0]  the I/O OCR
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_R4_READY     = $80000000     ; MMC_CARD_BUSY            m:206
#SDIO_R4_NFUNC     = $70000000     ; core/sdio.c:1269
#SDIO_R4_MEMORY    = $08000000     ; R4_MEMORY_PRESENT (1<<27) s:38
#SDIO_R4_18V       = $01000000     ; R4_18V_PRESENT    (1<<24) s:37
#SDIO_R4_OCR_MASK  = $00FFFFFF

; The voltage window asked for in the second CMD5. 3.2-3.4V, which is
; what this board supplies and all this file will run at - see "NO
; HIGH SPEED, NO UHS, NO 1.8V SWITCH". Bit n of the OCR is the window
; (n+15)/10 .. (n+16)/10 volts; bits 20 and 21 are 3.2-3.3 and 3.3-3.4.
#SDIO_OCR_32_34 = $00300000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  CCCR - the function-0 common registers.
;  v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio.h, line numbers given.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_CCCR_CCCR    = $00           ; s:67
#SDIO_CCCR_IOEx    = $02           ; s:87   function enable
#SDIO_CCCR_IORx    = $03           ; s:88   function ready
#SDIO_CCCR_IENx    = $04           ; s:90   interrupt enable
#SDIO_CCCR_INTx    = $05           ; s:91
#SDIO_CCCR_ABORT   = $06           ; s:93   abort / card reset
#SDIO_CCCR_IF      = $07           ; s:95   bus interface control
#SDIO_CCCR_CAPS    = $08           ; s:108
#SDIO_CCCR_SPEED   = $13           ; s:134

#SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_MASK = $03         ; SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_MASK      s:97
#SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_1BIT = $00         ; s:98
#SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_4BIT = $02         ; s:100
#SDIO_BUS_CD_DISABLE = $80         ; s:106

; The abort/reset bit. sdio_ops.c lines 209-215, sdio_reset(): read
; CCCR_ABORT, OR in $08, write it back; if the read failed, write a bare
; $08. Its comment cites "SDIO Simplified Specification V2.0, 4.4 Reset
; for SDIO".
#SDIO_ABORT_RES    = $08

; FBR - the per-function block-size registers.
; SDIO_FBR_BASE(f) = f * 0x100  (s:171), SDIO_FBR_BLKSIZE = 0x10 (s:192),
; two bytes, little end first.
#SDIO_FBR_STEP     = $100
#SDIO_FBR_BLKSIZE  = $10

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Clocks. Both of these are ceilings, so the divisor rounds UP.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_CLK_IDENT_KHZ = 400          ; identification. The SD physical
                                   ; layer's 400 kHz limit, the same
                                   ; number emmc.pi4 uses.
#SDIO_CLK_XFER_KHZ  = 25000        ; Default Speed. Not 50 MHz: high
                                   ; speed is not enabled here.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Timeouts, in milliseconds. Every wait in this file is bounded.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_TMO_RESET_MS   = 100
#SDIO_TMO_CLOCK_MS   = 100
#SDIO_TMO_INHIBIT_MS = 500
#SDIO_TMO_CMD_MS     = 200
#SDIO_TMO_DATA_MS    = 500
; CMD5 polling. The SDIO card has to bring its own regulators up. Linux
; gives it 100 tries at 10 ms apart (sdio_ops.c lines 27-53), so one
; second is the number that source implies.
#SDIO_TMO_OPCOND_MS  = 1000
; sdio_enable_func's default enable timeout in Linux comes from the
; function's CIS; with no CIS parser here, this is a choice, and it is
; generous on purpose.
#SDIO_TMO_ENABLE_MS  = 1000

; CNTFRQ_EL0 sanity bound. Same shape as safety.pi4's.
#SDIO_HZ_MAX = 1000000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The radio's power cycle, in milliseconds. BENCH-PROVEN NUMBERS, from
;  a different SoC and the same part family:
;  RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico lines 1990-1992, Cyw43Power().
;
;  20 ms low, because ">= 10 ms guarantees the regulator has actually
;  dropped, so a warm restart looks like a cold one" - that file's own
;  comment at line 1973. 150 ms high, because it is "the largest of the
;  three numbers the datasheet gives (3/50/150)" - line 1975.
;
;  The Pi 4 reaches the enable pin through the firmware's expander
;  rather than through a GPIO, so each edge here costs a property
;  transaction; the WAITS are the part that came from the proven driver
;  and the waits are what the regulator cares about.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_WL_ON_LOW_MS  = 20
#SDIO_WL_ON_HIGH_MS = 150

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  GPIO, for the pin mux only. See THE PINS.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_GPFSEL3 = $FE20000C          ; GPIO base $FE200000 + 3*4. Pins
                                   ; 30..39; 34..39 are bits 12..29.
#SDIO_GPPUP2  = $FE2000EC          ; GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2, base + $EC.
                                   ; Pins 32..47; 34..39 are bits 4..15.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE PULL VALUES, IN THE HARDWARE'S OWN ENCODING AND NOT THE DEVICE
;  TREE'S. THESE TWO NUMBERINGS ARE INVERTED. See THE PULL ENCODING IS
;  INVERTED in the header for what that cost.
;
;  bcm2711-peripherals.txt lines 6315-6318, Table 90 at line 6304:
;      00 = No resistor is selected
;      01 = Pull up resistor is selected
;      10 = Pull down resistor is selected
;      11 = Reserved
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_PULL_NONE = 0
#SDIO_PULL_UP   = 1
#SDIO_PULL_DOWN = 2

; ======================================================================
;  THE FIRMWARE PROPERTY TAGS THIS FILE ISSUES ITSELF
;
;  Through mailbox.pi4's escape hatch, not through any wrapper. See THE
;  POWER RAIL and IS THE CONTROLLER ITSELF POWER-GATED in the header for
;  the whole argument; these are just the numbers.
;
;  Tag numbers: Datasheets/pi4/hdmi/raspberrypi-firmware-downstream.h,
;  line given against each. The same numbers appear in
;  Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.h for the four that file carries.
;
;  Payload sizes: one size serves as both the request length and the
;  response buffer length - raspberrypi-firmware.c line 172,
;  "header->buf_size = buf_size" - and it is sizeof() of the struct.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_TAG_GET_POWER_STATE  = $00020001   ; downstream h:52
#SDIO_TAG_SET_POWER_STATE  = $00028001   ; downstream h:54
#SDIO_TAG_GET_CLOCK_STATE  = $00030001   ; downstream h:55
#SDIO_TAG_SET_CLOCK_STATE  = $00038001   ; downstream h:81
#SDIO_TAG_GET_GPIO_STATE   = $00030041   ; downstream h:87
#SDIO_TAG_SET_GPIO_STATE   = $00038041   ; downstream h:88
#SDIO_TAG_GET_GPIO_CONFIG  = $00030043   ; downstream h:90
#SDIO_TAG_SET_GPIO_CONFIG  = $00038043   ; downstream h:91

; struct gpio_set_config, six words - expander driver lines 32-39.
#SDIO_MBX_SET_CFG_BYTES = 24
; struct gpio_get_config, FIVE words, no trailing state - lines 41-47.
; This is the size the diagnostic had wrong on its first bench run.
#SDIO_MBX_GET_CFG_BYTES = 20
; struct gpio_get_set_state, two words - lines 49-52. Also the shape of
; both power-state tags and both clock-state tags: id then state.
#SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS = 8

; RPI_EXP_GPIO_BASE, expander driver line 20, used at line 111 as
; "off + RPI_EXP_GPIO_BASE".
#SDIO_EXP_GPIO_BASE = 128
; Expander line 1 is "WL_ON" - bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dts lines 61-70, where
; line 0 is "BT_ON" and line 1 is the next one named.
#SDIO_EXP_LINE_WL_ON = 1
; 128 + 1. Written out rather than computed so that a grep for the
; number people actually quote finds something.
#SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO = 129
; RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_OUT, expander driver line 23. DIR_IN is 0, line 22 -
; and note that the two are 0 and 1 in that order, which is the opposite
; of the way most GPIO blocks on this chip encode direction.
#SDIO_EXP_DIR_OUT = 1

; Mailbox power device id 0. Named SDHCI by
; Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h line 177 and "SD Card" by
; firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md line 168. See the header: this
; file queries it and only ever turns it ON.
#SDIO_PWR_DEVID_SDHCI = 0
; The highest device id any source on this disk names - the property
; document stops at 10 and calls 9 and 10 "Unknown (RPi4)", lines
; 177-178. The diagnostic sweeps 0..10 for that reason.
#SDIO_PWR_DEVID_MAX = 10

; Mailbox clock id 1, EMMC - v2025.01_bcm283x_mbox.h line 229. This
; controller's clock. NOT 12, which is EMMC2's.
#SDIO_CLOCK_ID_EMMC = 1

; The state words. Bit 0 is on/off in every one of these four tags; bit
; 1 means "does not exist" in a RESPONSE and "wait for the rail to
; settle" in a SET_POWER_STATE REQUEST. Two different meanings for one
; bit position in one tag pair, which is why they are named separately.
; firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md lines 187-193 (get power
; response), 212-232 (set power request and response), 257-292 (clock
; state, both directions).
#SDIO_MBX_STATE_ON     = 1
#SDIO_MBX_STATE_NODEV  = 2
#SDIO_MBX_PWRREQ_WAIT  = 2

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Error codes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#SDIO_ERR_NONE          = 0
#SDIO_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ     = 1
#SDIO_ERR_NO_CONTROLLER = 2
#SDIO_ERR_BAD_VERSION   = 3
; RETIRED 2026-08-26, NOT RENUMBERED. Nothing raises this any more; see
; THE CAPABILITIES REGISTER READS ZERO in the header. The slot stays
; occupied because every code below it appears in bench logs already
; filed and in tools/a64/a64_sdio_check.py.
#SDIO_ERR_NO_CAPS       = 4
#SDIO_ERR_BASECLK       = 5
#SDIO_ERR_RESET         = 6
#SDIO_ERR_CLOCK         = 7
#SDIO_ERR_INHIBIT       = 8
#SDIO_ERR_CMD_TIMEOUT   = 9
#SDIO_ERR_CMD_ERROR     = 10
#SDIO_ERR_CMD_STUCK     = 11
#SDIO_ERR_NO_CARD       = 12
#SDIO_ERR_NO_IO_FUNCS   = 13
#SDIO_ERR_OPCOND_TMO    = 14
#SDIO_ERR_CMD3          = 15
#SDIO_ERR_BAD_RCA       = 16
#SDIO_ERR_CMD7          = 17
#SDIO_ERR_R5            = 18
#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC      = 19
#SDIO_ERR_BAD_ADDR      = 20
#SDIO_ERR_BAD_LEN       = 21
#SDIO_ERR_ALIGN         = 22
#SDIO_ERR_NULL_BUF      = 23
#SDIO_ERR_DATA_TMO      = 24
#SDIO_ERR_DATA_ERROR    = 25
#SDIO_ERR_XFER_TMO      = 26
#SDIO_ERR_BUSY_TMO      = 27
#SDIO_ERR_NOT_READY     = 28
#SDIO_ERR_ENABLE_TMO    = 29
#SDIO_ERR_BUS_WIDTH     = 30
; Added 2026-08-26 with block-mode CMD53. A SEPARATE code from
; #SDIO_ERR_BAD_LEN on purpose: "your block size is wrong" and "your
; block count is wrong" send a caller to two different lines of their
; own code, and one shared code would have made the block-mode
; arithmetic harder to debug than the transfer it describes.
#SDIO_ERR_BAD_COUNT     = 31

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  State. Readable through accessors; not meant to be poked at.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global sdio_ready.i    = 0
Global sdio_err.i      = #SDIO_ERR_NONE
Global sdio_lastCmd.i  = -1
Global sdio_lastIntr.i = 0
Global sdio_lastStat.i = 0
Global sdio_lastR5.i   = 0

; Which block of a multi-block CMD53 was in flight when it went wrong,
; counted from 1, or 0 if no block-mode transfer has failed. A 511-block
; transfer that dies has to say WHERE: "block 1" is a command or block-
; size fault and "block 437" is a starved FIFO or a timeout budget that
; is too tight, and those are not the same bug. Left alone by a
; successful transfer so it can be read after the fact.
Global sdio_lastXferBlock.i = 0

Global sdio_hz.i        = 0    ; CNTFRQ_EL0
Global sdio_tickPerMs.i = 0

Global sdio_baseKhz.i  = 0
Global sdio_userBase.i = 0
Global sdio_clkKhz.i   = 0
Global sdio_divisor.i  = 0

Global sdio_ver.i    = 0       ; Host Controller spec version field
Global sdio_vendor.i = 0
Global sdio_caps0.i  = 0
Global sdio_caps1.i  = 0
Global sdio_hostVerRaw.i = 0   ; the whole word at $FC, as found
Global sdio_gpfsel3.i = 0      ; GPFSEL3 as sdio_MuxPins left it
Global sdio_gppup2.i  = 0      ; GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2, likewise

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  What the firmware said, and whether it was asked at all.
;
;  EVERY ONE OF THESE STARTS AT -1 AND -1 MEANS "NOT ATTEMPTED", which
;  is a different fact from "the firmware answered 0". A run with no
;  MailboxInit() must be distinguishable in the log from a run where the
;  firmware said the clock is off, and a single 0 cannot carry both.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global sdio_mbxUsed.i    = 0    ; 1 if any property tag was sent
Global sdio_wlOnStep.i   = -1   ; how far probe/assert got: see
                                ; SdioWlOnStep for the ladder
Global sdio_clkState.i   = -1   ; GET_CLOCK_STATE reply word 1, as found
Global sdio_clkStateAfter.i = -1 ; ... and after any SET
Global sdio_pwrState.i   = -1   ; GET_POWER_STATE(0) reply word 1
Global sdio_pwrStateAfter.i = -1
Global sdio_wlOnPolarity.i = -1 ; what GET_GPIO_CONFIG said, and what
                                ; was written back unchanged
Global sdio_wlOnLevel.i  = -1   ; GET_GPIO_STATE readback after the set
Global sdio_wlOnDirFound.i = -1 ; the direction GET_GPIO_CONFIG reported
Global sdio_wlOnLevelFound.i = -1  ; the level GET_GPIO_STATE reported
                                ; BEFORE anything was written to the pin
Global sdio_wlOnMbxErr.i = -1   ; MailboxError() captured at the rung
                                ; that stopped the ladder, so that a
                                ; refused tag and a timed-out transport
                                ; are distinguishable in the log
; SET_GPIO_CONFIG is ADVISORY as of the second bench run - it came back
; as #MBX_ERR_TAG while both GETs on the same pin passed. Whether that
; is a refusal or a misread success is OPEN - see AN OPEN QUESTION in
; the header. These record what happened, either way.
Global sdio_wlOnCfgOk.i  = -1
Global sdio_wlOnCfgErr.i = -1
; MailboxRespBytes() captured straight after each set-type tag: the
; length field out of that tag's own code word. THIS IS THE
; DISCRIMINATOR for the open question in the header - a zero length
; beside a cleared response bit is what a legitimately empty reply looks
; like, and eight bytes beside a cleared bit is not.
Global sdio_wlOnCfgResp.i  = -1
Global sdio_wlOnSetResp.i  = -1
Global sdio_clkSetResp.i   = -1
; 1 once WL_ON has been asserted SUCCESSFULLY in this run. It makes the
; second call a no-op, and that matters more than it looks - see the
; ONCE PER RUN paragraph above SdioAssertWlOn.
Global sdio_wlOnDone.i   = 0

; What SET_CLOCK_STATE actually DID, as opposed to what the state looked
; like afterwards. "The state did not change" and "the firmware refused
; the request" are different findings with different next steps, and the
; first bench log could not tell them apart.
Global sdio_clkSetReply.i = -1  ; the reply's state word, or -1 if the
                                ; transaction never completed at all
Global sdio_clkSetErr.i   = -1  ; MailboxError() straight after it
Global sdio_clkMeasured.i = -1  ; GET_CLOCK_MEASURED for clock 1

Global sdio_ocr.i    = 0       ; the I/O OCR the card published
Global sdio_nfunc.i  = 0       ; I/O functions, 1..7
Global sdio_mem.i    = 0       ; memory present bit
Global sdio_18v.i    = 0       ; 1.8V present bit - reported, never used
Global sdio_rca.i    = 0
Global sdio_width4.i = 0       ; 1 once the bus is four bits wide

Global sdio_resp0.i = 0
Global sdio_resp1.i = 0
Global sdio_resp2.i = 0
Global sdio_resp3.i = 0

; CMD53 service accounting. This is deliberately transport-neutral and
; measurement-only: the counters are never consulted by a transfer. Two
; counter reads per command bound its whole duration; the ready/end samples
; reuse the timer already read by the timeout loops. Linux brcmfmac exposes
; the analogous f2rxhdrs/f2rxdata/f2txdata and error counters.
Global sdio_cmd53Calls.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53ReadCalls.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53WriteCalls.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53Bytes.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53BlockCalls.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53ByteCalls.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53Failures.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53Ticks.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53MaxTicks.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53EndTicks.i = 0
Global sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks.i = 0


; ======================================================================
;  The clock. Two instructions each. Same form as emmc.pi4 and
;  safety.pi4, and for the same reason: these registers need no setup,
;  so a driver that must bound every wait can do it without owing
;  anything to another file.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i sdio_Ticks()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntpct_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i sdio_TickHz()
  ASM
    mrs x0, cntfrq_el0
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; Spin for approximately n microseconds. Used only where a settling
; delay is required and there is no status bit to poll instead. It is
; never a substitute for a poll.
Procedure sdio_DelayUs(us.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define want.i
  If sdio_hz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  want = (sdio_hz / 1000000) * us
  If want < 1
    want = 1
  EndIf
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  While (sdio_Ticks() - t0) < want
  Wend
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  Register access. THIRTY-TWO BITS, ALWAYS - see rule 1 in the header.
;
;  PeekN, not PeekL. PeekN is the unsigned 32-bit read; PeekL is signed
;  and .i is 64 bits on this target, so PeekL on a status word with bit
;  31 set comes back negative and every subsequent mask and compare is
;  wrong. Half the bits this file cares about live above bit 15.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i sdio_Rd(off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekN(#SDIO_BASE + off)
EndProcedure

Procedure sdio_Wr(off.i, v.i)
  PokeL(#SDIO_BASE + off, v)
EndProcedure

; Read-modify-write one byte lane of a 32-bit register, which is how
; every 8-bit SDHCI register on this block has to be written. Modelled
; on bcm2835_mmc_writeb, rpi-6.12.y_bcm2835-mmc.c lines 178-186.
Procedure sdio_WrByte(off.i, v.i)
  Define word.i
  Define shift.i
  Define cur.i
  word = off & (~3)
  shift = (off & 3) * 8
  cur = sdio_Rd(word)
  cur = cur & (~($FF << shift))
  cur = cur | ((v & $FF) << shift)
  sdio_Wr(word, cur)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  Failure bookkeeping. One place, so every refusal captures the same
;  three things: which error, the raw interrupt word, and the raw
;  Present State. A report naming only the error code sends somebody to
;  look at the wiring when the answer was in the status bits.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i sdio_Fail(code.i)
  sdio_err      = code
  sdio_lastIntr = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_INT_STATUS)
  sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_MuxPins - GPIO 34..39 to ALT3, with the device tree's pulls.
;
;  READ-MODIFY-WRITE, and the masks matter. GPFSEL3 holds pins 30..39;
;  writing the whole register would return GPIO 30..33 to plain inputs,
;  and on a Pi 4 those four are the Bluetooth UART's CTS/RTS pair and
;  two of the SD card's own lines on some board variants. Six pins to
;  change, four to leave alone.
;
;  ALT3 = 7. Not 3. See THE PINS in the header - this is the single
;  easiest number in this file to get wrong, because the device tree
;  spells it "ALT3" and the encoding is 0b111.
;
;  THE PULLS ARE THE DEVICE TREE'S, NOT A CHOICE: <0 2 2 2 2 2> from
;  rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711-rpi-ds.dtsi line 320, which is OFF on the clock
;  and UP on CMD and the four data lines. uart.pi4 makes its pull
;  decisions itself and says so; this file does not, because unlike a
;  console UART there IS a vendor group for these six pins and
;  second-guessing it would be inventing.
; ======================================================================
Procedure sdio_MuxPins()
  Define v.i
  Define i.i

  ; --- function select: six fields of three bits, at 12, 15, 18, 21,
  ;     24 and 27. Clear bits 29:12 ($3FFFF000), then set 7 in each.
  v = PeekN(#SDIO_GPFSEL3)
  v = v & (~$3FFFF000)
  For i = 0 To 5
    v = v | (7 << (12 + i * 3))
  Next
  PokeL(#SDIO_GPFSEL3, v)

  ; --- pulls: six fields of two bits, at 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 and 14.
  ;     GPIO34 = b00 (no resistor), GPIO35..39 = b01 (PULL UP).
  ;     Clear bits 15:4 ($FFF0), then set the five fields.
  ;
  ;     THE VALUE IS 1 AND IT USED TO BE 2. See THE PULL ENCODING IS
  ;     INVERTED in the header: on a BCM2711, b01 is pull-UP and b10 is
  ;     pull-DOWN, which is the opposite way round from the device
  ;     tree's BCM2835_PUD_* numbering that the source group is written
  ;     in. Writing the device tree's 2 here put a pull-DOWN on CMD and
  ;     on all four DAT lines.
  v = PeekN(#SDIO_GPPUP2)
  v = v & (~$0000FFF0)
  For i = 1 To 5
    v = v | (#SDIO_PULL_UP << (4 + i * 2))
  Next
  PokeL(#SDIO_GPPUP2, v)
  ; Recorded so the diagnostic can print what was actually programmed.
  ; A pin-mux that is asserted rather than shown is a pin-mux nobody
  ; checks, and this register is now known to be worth checking.
  sdio_gppup2 = PeekN(#SDIO_GPPUP2)
  sdio_gpfsel3 = PeekN(#SDIO_GPFSEL3)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE FIRMWARE HALF OF THE BRING-UP
;
;  Everything from here to SdioAssertWlOn() talks to the VideoCore
;  through mailbox.pi4's public escape hatch. Read THE POWER RAIL and IS
;  THE CONTROLLER ITSELF POWER-GATED in the header before changing any
;  of it; the numbers all have citations there and none of them was
;  recalled.
;
;  THREE RULES HOLD FOR EVERY PROCEDURE BELOW.
;
;  1. NOT ASKING AND BEING TOLD NO ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS. A query
;     returns -1 when the transaction did not happen at all - no
;     MailboxInit(), a buffer that would not lay out, a send that never
;     saw a reply - and returns the firmware's own word otherwise, even
;     when that word is 0. The globals start at -1 for the same reason.
;     A log that cannot tell "we never asked" from "it said off" sends
;     the next person to the wrong half of the board.
;
;  2. NOTHING HERE IS FATAL. The mailbox is not this file's subject. A
;     firmware that refuses every tag should still leave the register
;     work below it running and reporting, because the register work is
;     what this file is for and because a reader needs to see how far it
;     got.
;
;  3. NOTHING HERE EVER TURNS ANYTHING OFF. Not a clock, not a power
;     device, not a GPIO - except WL_ON, deliberately, for the 20 ms of
;     its own power cycle. See the header for why an "off" sent to power
;     device id 0 could be an "off" sent to the boot medium.
; ======================================================================


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_EnsureTimebase - make sdio_DelayUs work before SdioInit has run.
;
;  sdio_DelayUs returns immediately when sdio_hz is zero, which is
;  correct - a delay loop with no idea how fast the counter runs is not
;  a delay - but it means the WL_ON power cycle would be instantaneous
;  if a caller asserted WL_ON before SdioInit. SdioInit sets these two
;  as its first act; this makes the public entry point safe as well, and
;  it is idempotent.
;
;  Returns 1 if the counter is usable, 0 if it is not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i sdio_EnsureTimebase()
  If sdio_hz > 0 And sdio_tickPerMs > 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  sdio_hz = sdio_TickHz()
  If sdio_hz <= 0 Or sdio_hz > #SDIO_HZ_MAX
    sdio_hz = 0
    sdio_tickPerMs = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_tickPerMs = sdio_hz / 1000
  If sdio_tickPerMs < 1
    sdio_hz = 0
    sdio_tickPerMs = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_DelayMs - milliseconds, for the radio's power cycle.
;
;  sdio_DelayUs multiplies by (sdio_hz / 1000000), which on this part is
;  54 ticks per microsecond, so a 150 ms argument in microseconds is
;  150000 and the product is well inside 64 bits. It is written as its
;  own procedure anyway because the two call sites read as milliseconds
;  in every source they came from and translating at each of them is how
;  a factor of a thousand goes missing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure sdio_DelayMs(ms.i)
  sdio_DelayUs(ms * 1000)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_MbxIdState - the shape four of these eight tags share.
;
;  GET_POWER_STATE, SET_POWER_STATE, GET_CLOCK_STATE and SET_CLOCK_STATE
;  all carry { u32 id, u32 state } in both directions, eight bytes -
;  firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md lines 180-232 for the power
;  pair and 257-292 for the clock pair. A GET's documented request
;  length is 4 rather than 8, but the buffer still has to be eight bytes
;  wide to receive the reply, and the firmware is handed ONE size for
;  both purposes (raspberrypi-firmware.c line 172). Eight is that size.
;  MailboxBegin zeroes the value words, so the unsent second word of a
;  GET goes out as 0 rather than as whatever the last tag left there.
;
;  sendState = 0 for a query, 1 for a set.
;
;  Returns the reply's state word, or -1 if the transaction did not
;  complete. Rule 1 above.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i sdio_MbxIdState(tag.i, id.i, state.i, sendState.i)
  If MailboxReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  sdio_mbxUsed = 1
  If MailboxBegin(tag, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS) = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, id)
  If sendState <> 0
    MailboxSetWord(1, state)
  EndIf
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn MailboxWord(1)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioQueryPowerState(id) - GET_POWER_STATE for one device id.
;
;  PUBLIC, because the one thing no file on this disk can tell us is
;  which physical controller device id 0 gates on a BCM2711, and a sweep
;  of 0 through #SDIO_PWR_DEVID_MAX answers it in one bench run. The
;  diagnostic prints that table.
;
;  Returns the state word: bit 0 on/off, bit 1 set means the firmware
;  has no such device. -1 if it was not asked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioQueryPowerState(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_MbxIdState(#SDIO_TAG_GET_POWER_STATE, id, 0, 0)
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioQueryClockState(id) - GET_CLOCK_STATE for one clock id.
;
;  PUBLIC for the same reason, and it is the tag that answers the
;  question the first bench run could not: GET_CLOCK_RATE returned
;  250000000 for clock 1, and the property document says at line 310
;  that a rate comes back "even if the clock is not running". Bit 0 of
;  this reply is the running/not-running fact itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioQueryClockState(id.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_MbxIdState(#SDIO_TAG_GET_CLOCK_STATE, id, 0, 0)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  WHAT THE SECOND BENCH RUN SAID ABOUT THESE STATE TAGS, AND WHY THIS
;  PROCEDURE NO LONGER TOUCHES THE POWER DEVICES
;  ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The board ran the sweep on 2026-08-26 and answered:
;
;      power devid 0        state 1   exists, ON
;      power devid 1..10    state 0   exists, off   (all ten of them)
;
;      EMMC  clock (id 1)   state 0   off
;      EMMC  clock measured 250000496 Hz
;      EMMC2 clock (id 12)  state 1   on
;
;  READ DEVID 1 AGAIN. Device id 1 is UART0 - the property document's
;  own list, firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md line 169. UART0 is
;  the PL011 that PRINTED THAT LINE. The firmware reported a peripheral
;  as powered off in a message the peripheral was in the middle of
;  transmitting. That is not a subtle inference; it is the log
;  contradicting itself in two adjacent rows.
;
;  SO THE POWER TABLE IS NOT MAINTAINED ON A BCM2711 and this procedure
;  will not act on it. It reads devid 0, records it, and sends nothing.
;  Acting on a table that calls a transmitting UART "off" is acting on
;  noise, and the one entry that might have mattered - devid 0 - came
;  back ON anyway, so there was never anything to switch. The sweep
;  stays in the diagnostic because the shape of the answer is itself the
;  finding, and because a future firmware might start maintaining it.
;
;  AND THE CLOCK ROW CONTRADICTS ITSELF THE SAME WAY. GET_CLOCK_STATE
;  says clock 1 is off; GET_CLOCK_MEASURED says it is running at
;  250000496 Hz. That is a MEASUREMENT - the firmware counts edges to
;  produce it, which is the whole reason the tag exists separately from
;  GET_CLOCK_RATE (see THE BASE CLOCK) - and it carries 496 Hz of real
;  jitter off the nominal 250 MHz. A gated clock cannot be measured at
;  250.000496 MHz. Something is generating that clock.
;
;  THE HONEST READING, and it is a reading and not a citation: on this
;  part the state bit appears to mean "some client has claimed this
;  clock" rather than "the generator is running", exactly as the power
;  bits appear to mean nothing at all. EMMC2 reads state 1 because the
;  firmware's own SD driver claimed it at boot; nothing has claimed the
;  Arasan block's clock because on a Pi 4 nothing in the firmware uses
;  that controller.
;
;  THE SET IS STILL ATTEMPTED, EVERY RUN. Two reasons. The reading above
;  could be wrong, and it costs one transaction to find out. And if the
;  firmware refuses the tag outright then that refusal is a fact worth
;  having in the log, which it was not before - the first run recorded
;  only that the state had not changed, which is compatible with the set
;  never being sent, being sent and refused, or being accepted and
;  ignored. Those now print differently.
;
;  ---- AND THE BOARD SETTLED IT, 2026-08-26 ---------------------------
;  It was sent, it was ACCEPTED, and the state did not move:
;
;      clock state before   0
;      clock set reply      1        the firmware replied "on"
;      clock set mbx err    0        MBX_OK - the transaction completed
;      clock state after    0        and the state bit is still off
;      clock Hz measured    249987312
;
;  So the third of the three stories. Not a refusal - SET_CLOCK_STATE
;  passed mailbox.pi4's per-tag check and answered "on" in its own reply
;  word. The state bit simply does not become 1 and stay 1.
;
;  AND THAT FACT IS NOW LOAD-BEARING SOMEWHERE ELSE. It is the strongest
;  evidence this file holds on the open question about the two expander
;  set tags: a set-type tag DID pass the per-tag check on this firmware
;  in this run, so "mailbox.pi4 is too strict about set-type tags"
;  cannot by itself explain why the two GPIO sets did not. See AN OPEN
;  QUESTION in the header, and do not let this paragraph drift from
;  that one.
;
;  AND THE CLOCK IS RUNNING THE WHOLE TIME. 249987312 Hz measured, on a
;  nominal 250 MHz: 12688 Hz low, about fifty parts per million, which
;  is a crystal's tolerance and not a rounding artefact. Two separate
;  runs measured two different values - 250000496 the first time and
;  249987312 this time - which is what independent measurements of a
;  live oscillator look like and is not what a constant returned from a
;  table looks like.
;
;  THE READING ABOVE IS THEREFORE CONFIRMED, by three witnesses that
;  agree: the state bit means SOMETHING HAS CLAIMED THIS CLOCK, not
;  "the generator is turning". Clock 12 reads claimed because the
;  firmware's own SD driver claimed it at boot; clock 1 reads unclaimed
;  because nothing in the firmware drives the Arasan block; the counter
;  says both are turning. The power table said the same thing about a
;  transmitting UART0. And the controller itself is the fourth witness -
;  SdioInit() gets past sdio_SetClock(), which spins on Internal Clock
;  Stable in the block's own CONTROL1 register, and that bit cannot come
;  up in an unclocked block.
;
;  NOTHING FURTHER IS OWED TO THIS. The clock question is closed: the
;  block is clocked. The set stays because it is cheap and honest, and
;  because a firmware that starts honouring it would show up here as a
;  change rather than as a mystery.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_EnableClockAndPower - ask about the EMMC clock, try to claim it,
;  and record precisely what happened. Read the power device; set
;  nothing.
;
;  Records everything and returns nothing. Rule 2 - nothing here is
;  fatal and nothing below depends on any of it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure sdio_EnableClockAndPower()
  Define s.i

  ; --- the clock -------------------------------------------------------
  ; GET_CLOCK_STATE, eight bytes: { u32 clock id, u32 state }.
  ; firmware-Mailbox-property-interface.md lines 257-271.
  s = SdioQueryClockState(#SDIO_CLOCK_ID_EMMC)
  sdio_clkState = s
  sdio_clkStateAfter = s

  ; The second witness, and the one that contradicted the first. Already
  ; wrapped by mailbox.pi4, so no escape hatch is needed for it.
  sdio_clkMeasured = MailboxClockRateMeasured(#SDIO_CLOCK_ID_EMMC)

  If s >= 0
    If (s & #SDIO_MBX_STATE_NODEV) = 0 And (s & #SDIO_MBX_STATE_ON) = 0
      ; SET_CLOCK_STATE, eight bytes, same two words in both directions -
      ; same document, lines 273-292. State bit 0 = 1 is "on"; bit 1 in a
      ; REQUEST is reserved here and is left clear, which is what line
      ; 283 asks for ("reserved for future use (set to 0)").
      sdio_clkSetReply = sdio_MbxIdState(#SDIO_TAG_SET_CLOCK_STATE, #SDIO_CLOCK_ID_EMMC, #SDIO_MBX_STATE_ON, 1)
      ; CAPTURED IMMEDIATELY, before any other transaction can overwrite
      ; it. MailboxError() is cleared at the start of every
      ; MailboxBegin(), so one more property call anywhere between the
      ; set and the read would erase the only evidence of why the set
      ; failed. That is the whole bug the bench asked to have fixed.
      sdio_clkSetErr  = MailboxError()
      sdio_clkSetResp = MailboxRespBytes()
      sdio_clkStateAfter = SdioQueryClockState(#SDIO_CLOCK_ID_EMMC)
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; --- the power device: READ ONLY -------------------------------------
  ; See the long comment above. Nothing is sent. #SDIO_TAG_SET_POWER_STATE
  ; and #SDIO_MBX_PWRREQ_WAIT stay declared because the sweep's printed
  ; explanation refers to them and because removing a cited constant to
  ; make an unused-symbol warning go away is how a citation gets lost.
  s = SdioQueryPowerState(#SDIO_PWR_DEVID_SDHCI)
  sdio_pwrState = s
  sdio_pwrStateAfter = s
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  WL_ON - WHAT THE SECOND BENCH RUN CHANGED HERE
;  ---------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The first version of this procedure did GET_GPIO_CONFIG, then
;  SET_GPIO_CONFIG with direction OUT and state LOW, then a 20 ms wait,
;  then SET_GPIO_STATE HIGH. The board ran it and stopped at rung 3,
;  which was "the polarity was read and SET_GPIO_CONFIG did not go".
;
;  SO THE ORDER WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS THE DEPENDENCY. Three separate
;  faults, all fixed below.
;
;  1. IT MADE THE WHOLE SEQUENCE DEPEND ON THE ONE TAG THE BOARD
;     REFUSES. GET_GPIO_CONFIG worked - it returned a polarity, and
;     word 0 came back 0, which is the firmware accepting pin 129. So
;     the pin number is right, the +128 base is right, the transport is
;     right, and the twenty-byte payload fix from this morning was
;     right. Only SET_GPIO_CONFIG failed. That tag is now ADVISORY: it
;     is still sent, its outcome is still recorded, and nothing waits
;     for it. The mandatory path is GET_GPIO_STATE, SET_GPIO_STATE,
;     GET_GPIO_STATE - three uses of ONE eight-byte struct, which is the
;     payload shape least likely to be the thing that is wrong.
;
;     This is also what the kernel does when it only wants to move a pin
;     it is not reconfiguring: rpi_exp_gpio_set(), expander driver lines
;     178-195, sends SET_GPIO_STATE and nothing else. WL_ON is a pin the
;     firmware already owns and has already configured as an output, so
;     the config call was always the belt and the state call the braces,
;     and this file had them the wrong way round.
;
;  2. IT DROVE THE PIN LOW BEFORE IT KNEW THE HIGH WOULD WORK. If the
;     low had gone through and the high had not, the radio would have
;     been left switched OFF by a procedure whose job is to switch it
;     on, and the log would have said "asserted 0" with no hint that it
;     had made things worse. THE LOW PHASE NOW ONLY HAPPENS WHEN THE
;     READBACK SAYS THE PIN IS ALREADY HIGH - which is the only case
;     where a power cycle is what is wanted anyway. A pin found low is
;     already in the state the cycle would put it in, so there is
;     nothing to cycle and nothing to risk: it is driven high and that
;     is the whole operation. On this board the pin was found low.
;
;     It also settles the Bluetooth worry recorded above: with the pin
;     found low there is no BT link to disturb, because there is no
;     radio powered to be carrying one.
;
;  3. RUNG 3 MEANT TWO DIFFERENT THINGS. It covered both "MailboxBegin
;     would not lay the message out" and "MailboxSend did not complete",
;     which have completely different causes - a buffer that will not
;     fit versus a firmware that refused. Every rung below is now one
;     event, and MailboxError() is captured at whichever rung stopped,
;     so a refused tag (#MBX_ERR_TAG) is distinguishable from a
;     transport timeout (#MBX_ERR_TXTIMEOUT) in the printed log.
;
;  THE READBACK IS NOW THE DEFINITION OF SUCCESS. This returns 1 only
;  when GET_GPIO_STATE says the pin is high afterwards. "The firmware
;  accepted my write" was never the claim worth making.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioAssertWlOn - power the radio, through the firmware's expander.
;
;  PUBLIC, and also called by SdioInit. The diagnostic calls it
;  explicitly first so that the ladder is printed before the bus work
;  starts, and SdioInit's later call is then a no-op.
;
;  ONCE PER RUN, AND THE GUARD IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION. "Calling it twice
;  is harmless" is what this comment used to say and it stopped being
;  true the moment the low phase became conditional on the pin being
;  found high. The first call leaves the pin HIGH; a second call
;  therefore finds it high, decides a warm radio needs a clean cycle,
;  and switches the radio off and on again - 170 ms later, with the chip
;  back at its power-on state, moments before CMD5. That is a self-
;  inflicted reset that no caller asked for, and the simulation caught
;  it: the gate flagged a LOW write on a board where the pin was found
;  LOW, which is exactly the footprint of a second cycle.
;
;  So sdio_wlOnDone latches on success and the second call returns 1
;  without sending anything, leaving every recorded value from the first
;  call intact for the log. SdioForgetWlOn() clears the latch for a
;  caller that genuinely wants the radio power-cycled again.
;
;  THE LADDER IS RECORDED IN sdio_wlOnStep AND EVERY RUNG HAS A SENTENCE
;  in SdioWlOnStepText(). That text lives in this file, not in a report,
;  because the person reading a serial log at midnight does not have the
;  report.
;
;  THE PAYLOAD SIZES, EVERY ONE OF THEM RE-CHECKED against the structs
;  after the first run got one wrong. rpi_firmware_property() hands the
;  firmware a single size that serves as both the request length and the
;  response buffer length - Datasheets/pi4/raspberrypi-firmware.c line
;  172, "header->buf_size = buf_size" - and that size is sizeof() of the
;  struct the kernel passes. From
;  Datasheets/pi4/rpi-linux/rpi-6.12.y_gpio-raspberrypi-exp.c:
;
;    struct gpio_set_config     lines 32-39  gpio, direction, polarity,
;                                            term_en, term_pull_up,
;                                            state
;                                            SIX words, 24 bytes.
;                                            Passed at line 93 and line
;                                            122 as sizeof(set_in) /
;                                            sizeof(set_out).
;
;    struct gpio_get_config     lines 41-47  gpio, direction, polarity,
;                                            term_en, term_pull_up
;                                            FIVE words, 20 bytes - no
;                                            trailing state. Passed at
;                                            line 64 and line 142 as
;                                            sizeof(get).
;
;    struct gpio_get_set_state  lines 49-52  gpio, state
;                                            TWO words, 8 bytes. Passed
;                                            at line 167 and line 189 as
;                                            sizeof(get) / sizeof(set).
;
;  Each call site below names its own size and its own struct again, so
;  that a reader checking one call does not have to trust this list.
;
;  THE POLARITY IS READ BACK AND WRITTEN BACK UNCHANGED, wherever it is
;  written at all. The expander driver does this at lines 117-120 with
;  the comment "Retain existing setting". Inventing a polarity is a
;  one-in-two chance of turning the radio off while reporting success.
;
;  DIRECTION IS 1, NOT 0. Expander driver lines 22-23:
;
;      #define RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_IN   0
;      #define RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_OUT  1
;
;  Returns 1 only if the readback says the pin is high.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioAssertWlOn()
  Define polarity.i
  Define level.i

  ; See ONCE PER RUN above. Everything the first call recorded is left
  ; exactly as it was, because that is what the diagnostic prints.
  If sdio_wlOnDone = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  sdio_wlOnStep       = 0
  sdio_wlOnLevel      = -1
  sdio_wlOnLevelFound = -1
  sdio_wlOnDirFound   = -1
  sdio_wlOnPolarity   = -1
  sdio_wlOnMbxErr     = -1
  sdio_wlOnCfgOk      = -1
  sdio_wlOnCfgErr     = -1
  sdio_EnsureTimebase()

  If MailboxReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_mbxUsed = 1

  ; --- rungs 1..3: GET_GPIO_CONFIG -------------------------------------
  ; struct gpio_get_config, FIVE words, 20 bytes. Expander driver lines
  ; 41-47, sent at line 64 as sizeof(get).
  sdio_wlOnStep = 1
  If MailboxBegin(#SDIO_TAG_GET_GPIO_CONFIG, #SDIO_MBX_GET_CFG_BYTES, #SDIO_MBX_GET_CFG_BYTES) = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, #SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO)
  sdio_wlOnStep = 2
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnStep = 3
  ; Word 0 comes back 0 on success. The expander driver tests exactly
  ; that - "if (ret || get.gpio != 0)", line 66 - because the firmware
  ; reports a rejected pin number by echoing it instead of clearing it.
  If MailboxWord(0) <> 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnDirFound = MailboxWord(1)
  polarity          = MailboxWord(2)
  sdio_wlOnPolarity = polarity

  ; --- rungs 4..6: GET_GPIO_STATE, the level as found ------------------
  ; struct gpio_get_set_state, TWO words, 8 bytes. Expander driver lines
  ; 49-52, sent at line 167 as sizeof(get).
  ;
  ; THIS READ IS WHY THE PROCEDURE IS SAFE. Everything after it depends
  ; on knowing whether there is a powered radio to cycle or a dead one
  ; to switch on, and those need different sequences.
  sdio_wlOnStep = 4
  If MailboxBegin(#SDIO_TAG_GET_GPIO_STATE, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS) = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, #SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO)
  sdio_wlOnStep = 5
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnStep = 6
  If MailboxWord(0) <> 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  level = MailboxWord(1)
  sdio_wlOnLevelFound = level

  ; --- THE SHORT CIRCUIT, AND ON THIS BOARD IT IS THE WHOLE ANSWER -----
  ; If the pin is ALREADY an output and ALREADY high, the radio is
  ; already powered and there is nothing to do. Land on the success rung
  ; with the level the read just returned and send no write at all.
  ;
  ; THE BOARD FAILED FOR WANT OF THIS, on 2026-08-26:
  ;
  ;     WL_ON dir as found     1     already an output
  ;     WL_ON level as found   1     already high
  ;     WL_ON step             8     SET_GPIO_STATE HIGH did not complete
  ;     WL_ON mailbox err      8     MBX_ERR_TAG
  ;
  ; The firmware had powered the radio at boot, we asked it to set a pin
  ; that was already set, the set came back as #MBX_ERR_TAG, and this
  ; procedure reported failure over a state of affairs that was exactly
  ; what it wanted. That is the worst kind of bug in a bring-up: it
  ; reports the goal as unreached while standing on it.
  ;
  ; AND THIS SHORT CIRCUIT IS RIGHT WHICHEVER WAY THE OPEN QUESTION IN
  ; THE HEADER GOES. If the firmware really does decline expander
  ; writes, skipping the write is the only thing that can work. If we
  ; have instead been misreading a successful set, skipping it is still
  ; correct, because the write was a no-op on an already-correct pin.
  ; A fix that does not depend on an unsettled question is a fix that
  ; can land while the question is unsettled.
  ;
  ; IT IS ALSO STRICTLY SAFER. On the common path there is now no write
  ; to the expander at all, so the sequence cannot disturb a radio that
  ; is already running, and the Bluetooth question raised further up
  ; stops being a question.
  If level = 1 And sdio_wlOnDirFound = #SDIO_EXP_DIR_OUT
    sdio_wlOnLevel = level
    sdio_wlOnStep  = 13
    sdio_wlOnDone  = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; --- ADVISORY: SET_GPIO_CONFIG, direction OUT ------------------------
  ; struct gpio_set_config, SIX words, 24 bytes. Expander driver lines
  ; 32-39, sent at line 122 as sizeof(set_out).
  ;
  ; THE STATE WORD IS THE LEVEL WE JUST READ, NOT A NEW ONE. This call
  ; is about the DIRECTION; making it also move the pin would give a tag
  ; the board reports as #MBX_ERR_TAG a say in whether the radio ends
  ; up powered. Writing back the level that is already there means the call
  ; changes nothing if it succeeds and nothing if it fails, which is
  ; what "advisory" has to mean to be worth sending at all.
  ;
  ; NOT FATAL, and no rung. This tag came back as #MBX_ERR_TAG on
  ; 2026-08-26 while both GETs on the same pin passed, and the sequence
  ; below does not need it either way. See AN OPEN QUESTION.
  sdio_wlOnCfgOk = 0
  If MailboxBegin(#SDIO_TAG_SET_GPIO_CONFIG, #SDIO_MBX_SET_CFG_BYTES, #SDIO_MBX_SET_CFG_BYTES) <> 0
    MailboxSetWord(0, #SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO)
    MailboxSetWord(1, #SDIO_EXP_DIR_OUT)
    MailboxSetWord(2, polarity)
    MailboxSetWord(3, 0)            ; term_en      - n/a for an output
    MailboxSetWord(4, 0)            ; term_pull_up - no termination
    MailboxSetWord(5, level)        ; state - unchanged, deliberately
    If MailboxSend() <> 0
      If MailboxWord(0) = 0
        sdio_wlOnCfgOk = 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnCfgErr  = MailboxError()
  sdio_wlOnCfgResp = MailboxRespBytes()

  ; --- the low phase, ONLY if there is something to cycle --------------
  ; See fault 2 in the block above. A pin found HIGH means a radio that
  ; has been running, and the Pico driver's reason for the cycle applies
  ; - ">= 10 ms guarantees the regulator has actually dropped, so a warm
  ; restart looks like a cold one", RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico line 1973. A
  ; pin found LOW is already where the cycle would put it, so the cycle
  ; is skipped and the pin is only ever driven towards ON.
  If level = 1
    If MailboxBegin(#SDIO_TAG_SET_GPIO_STATE, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS) <> 0
      MailboxSetWord(0, #SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO)
      MailboxSetWord(1, 0)
      MailboxSend()
    EndIf
    sdio_DelayMs(#SDIO_WL_ON_LOW_MS)
  EndIf

  ; --- rungs 7..9: SET_GPIO_STATE, HIGH. The one that matters ----------
  ; struct gpio_get_set_state again, TWO words, 8 bytes - expander
  ; driver lines 49-52, sent at line 189 as sizeof(set). This is
  ; rpi_exp_gpio_set()'s whole body.
  sdio_wlOnStep = 7
  If MailboxBegin(#SDIO_TAG_SET_GPIO_STATE, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS) = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, #SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO)
  MailboxSetWord(1, 1)
  sdio_wlOnStep = 8
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr  = MailboxError()
    sdio_wlOnSetResp = MailboxRespBytes()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnSetResp = MailboxRespBytes()
  sdio_wlOnStep = 9
  If MailboxWord(0) <> 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; 150 ms, "the largest of the three numbers the datasheet gives
  ; (3/50/150)" - RP2040/Lib/cyw43.pico line 1975, and that driver has
  ; run. Spent here whether or not the low phase happened, because it is
  ; the time the radio needs to come up, not the time the rail needs to
  ; fall.
  sdio_DelayMs(#SDIO_WL_ON_HIGH_MS)

  ; --- rungs 10..12: GET_GPIO_STATE, the readback ----------------------
  ; struct gpio_get_set_state, TWO words, 8 bytes, expander driver lines
  ; 49-52 and line 167.
  sdio_wlOnStep = 10
  If MailboxBegin(#SDIO_TAG_GET_GPIO_STATE, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS, #SDIO_MBX_TWO_WORDS) = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  MailboxSetWord(0, #SDIO_WL_ON_GPIO)
  sdio_wlOnStep = 11
  If MailboxSend() = 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnStep = 12
  If MailboxWord(0) <> 0
    sdio_wlOnMbxErr = MailboxError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_wlOnLevel = MailboxWord(1)
  sdio_wlOnStep = 13

  ; SUCCESS IS THE READBACK, not the acceptance. A firmware that took
  ; every write and still reports the pin low has told us something, and
  ; it is not "yes".
  If sdio_wlOnLevel = 1
    sdio_wlOnDone = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioForgetWlOn - clear the once-per-run latch so the next
;  SdioAssertWlOn() really does power-cycle the radio again.
;
;  Nothing in this project calls it. It exists because the latch is a
;  behaviour change and a latch with no way out is a trap: a caller
;  recovering from a wedged radio has a legitimate reason to want the
;  cycle repeated, and without this it would have to reboot the board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure SdioForgetWlOn()
  sdio_wlOnDone = 0
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_ControllerPlausible - is there an SDHCI controller at
;  #SDIO_BASE, and is it a DIFFERENT one from the SD card's?
;
;  The first half is emmc.pi4's sd_ControllerPlausible(), for the same
;  reason: a store to a real, powered, idle peripheral produces no
;  output and no error. Host Controller Version all-zero or all-ones
;  means nothing is decoding here; a specification-version field above 4
;  is not a version number, so this is not that register, so this is not
;  the controller.
;
;  IT USED TO CHECK CAPABILITIES AS WELL AND IT NO LONGER DOES. That
;  second check was the same argument from a different offset, and the
;  board proved the offset does not support the argument. Capabilities
;  is now read, recorded and reported. See the body.
;
;  THE SECOND HALF IS NEW AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN HAZARD. There are
;  two SDHCI-class controllers on this chip, $40000 apart, and a
;  one-digit slip in the base constant lands on the micro-SD card's
;  controller - which WILL answer all three checks above, perfectly,
;  because it is a real SDHCI controller. Everything after that would
;  then reset and reconfigure the boot medium while reporting success.
;
;  There is no register that says "I am the Arasan one and not the
;  emmc2 one". What there is:
;
;    * emmc2 is "brcm,bcm2711-emmc2" and reports a spec version of 3;
;      the Arasan block is older silicon. That is not a discriminator
;      that can be relied on and it is not used as one.
;
;    * WHAT IS USED: the CARD DETECT bits. The Arasan controller's
;      device-tree node is marked non-removable (bcm270x.dtsi line 58)
;      and the CYW43455 is soldered to it; emmc2 drives a socket. So
;      this records both Present State card bits AS FOUND and hands
;      them to the caller through SdioCardDetectBit() and
;      SdioCardPinLevel() rather than acting on them.
;
;  THAT IS A REPORT, NOT A GUARD, and calling it a guard would be a lie.
;  Nothing on this disk can distinguish the two blocks by register
;  content. The defence against a wrong base address is the citation
;  chain in the header, and the honest statement of the risk is here.
;
;  Returns 1 if it looks like a controller, 0 with sdio_err set.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_ControllerPlausible()
  Define v.i
  Define c.i

  ; SDHCI_HOST_VERSION is the 16-bit register at $FE, so it is the top
  ; half of the word at $FC. SDHCI_SPEC_VER_MASK $00FF and
  ; SDHCI_VENDOR_VER_MASK $FF00 (sdhci.h lines 280-283) apply to that
  ; halfword, hence the >> 16 first.
  v = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_HOST_VER)
  sdio_hostVerRaw = v
  If v = 0 Or v = $FFFFFFFF
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NO_CONTROLLER)
  EndIf
  sdio_ver    = (v >> 16) & $FF
  sdio_vendor = (v >> 24) & $FF
  If sdio_ver > 4
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_VERSION)
  EndIf

  ; CAPABILITIES IS RECORDED AND NOT JUDGED. This used to refuse with
  ; #SDIO_ERR_NO_CAPS when the register read all-zero, and on 2026-08-26
  ; the board read exactly that while $FE3000FC returned $99020000 - a
  ; perfectly good Host Controller Version - and the refusal sent the
  ; bench to check an address that was correct.
  ;
  ; The full argument is THE CAPABILITIES REGISTER READS ZERO HERE AND
  ; THAT IS NOT A FAULT, in the header. The short form: the driver
  ; Raspberry Pi binds to this node, rpi-6.12.y_bcm2835-mmc.c, mentions
  ; SDHCI_CAPABILITIES once in the entire file, at lines 255-257, inside
  ; a pr_debug register dump. It takes the base clock from the clock
  ; framework instead (line 1452), writes the 3.3V rail without asking
  ; whether the controller claims to support it (lines 1180-1182), and
  ; hard-codes its capability flags (lines 1302-1306). A register that
  ; the vendor's own driver reads only to print is not a register this
  ; file may refuse over.
  c = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CAPS0)
  sdio_caps0 = c
  sdio_caps1 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CAPS1)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  The base clock. See THE BASE CLOCK in the header. This is allowed to
;  say "I do not know" and it is the only number here that is.
;
;  sdhci.h lines 214-215: SDHCI_CLOCK_BASE_MASK is GENMASK(13, 8) -
;  bits 13:8 - at SDHCI_CLOCK_BASE_SHIFT 8, in MHz. Later versions of
;  the specification widened that field to bits 15:8 on a 3.00
;  controller; the Linux header carries only the narrow form, so the
;  narrow form is what is used, and a controller reporting more than 63
;  MHz through the wide field would come back truncated rather than
;  wrong-by-a-factor. Zero means "not obtainable here".
;
;  Returns kHz, or 0 for unknown.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_BaseClockKhz()
  Define mhz.i
  If sdio_userBase > 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_userBase
  EndIf
  mhz = (sdio_caps0 >> 8) & $3F
  If mhz = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn mhz * 1000
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_DivisorFor - the N the Clock Control register wants.
;
;  10-bit Divided Clock Mode: SDCLK = base / (2 * N), N = 0 meaning the
;  base clock undivided. The field is split - sdhci.h lines 119-123 put
;  N[7:0] at SDHCI_DIVIDER_SHIFT 8 and N[9:8] at SDHCI_DIVIDER_HI_SHIFT
;  6 - which is why sdio_SetClock below assembles it in two pieces.
;
;  Rounds N UP, always. Both frequencies this file asks for are
;  CEILINGS: 400 kHz is the identification limit and 25 MHz is Default
;  Speed. A bus a little slow works; a bus a little fast is out of spec.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_DivisorFor(baseKhz.i, wantKhz.i)
  Define n.i
  Define twice.i
  If wantKhz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn 1023
  EndIf
  If baseKhz <= wantKhz
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  twice = 2 * wantKhz
  n = (baseKhz + twice - 1) / twice
  If n < 1
    n = 1
  EndIf
  If n > 1023
    n = 1023
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn n
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_SetClock - stop the clock, program a divisor, start it again.
;
;  CONTROL1 IS REBUILT WHOLE rather than read-modify-written. The word
;  carries three registers - Clock Control, Timeout Control and Software
;  Reset - and a read-modify-write would carry whatever happened to be
;  in the Software Reset byte back into the register. Composing it from
;  scratch means the bits that go in are exactly the bits named here.
;
;  THE ORDER IS NOT NEGOTIABLE:
;    1. clear SD Clock Enable  (never change a divisor while the clock
;       is running - the card sees a runt pulse and loses sync)
;    2. write divisor + Internal Clock Enable
;    3. wait for Internal Clock Stable
;    4. set SD Clock Enable
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with sdio_err set if the clock never stabilised.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_SetClock(wantKhz.i)
  Define n.i
  Define c1.i
  Define lo.i
  Define hi.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i

  n = sdio_DivisorFor(sdio_baseKhz, wantKhz)
  sdio_divisor = n
  If n = 0
    sdio_clkKhz = sdio_baseKhz
  Else
    sdio_clkKhz = sdio_baseKhz / (2 * n)
  EndIf

  ; 1
  c1 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL1) & (~#SDIO_CLK_CARD_EN)
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL1, c1)

  ; 2. N[7:0] to word bits 15:8, N[9:8] to word bits 7:6.
  lo = (n & $FF) << 8
  hi = ((n >> 8) & 3) << 6
  c1 = #SDIO_TIMEOUT_MAX | lo | hi | #SDIO_CLK_INT_EN
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL1, c1)

  ; 3
  budget = #SDIO_TMO_CLOCK_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  While (sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL1) & #SDIO_CLK_INT_STABLE) = 0
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_CLOCK)
    EndIf
  Wend

  ; 4
  c1 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL1) | #SDIO_CLK_CARD_EN
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL1, c1)

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_WaitInhibit - do not issue a command into a busy controller.
;
;  Command Inhibit (CMD), Present State bit 0 (sdhci.h line 66), is set
;  while the CMD line is in use. Command Inhibit (DAT), bit 1 (line 67),
;  is set while the DAT line is in use or while the card is signalling
;  busy. A command issued while either is set is not queued - it is
;  lost, silently.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_WaitInhibit(needDat.i)
  Define mask.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  mask = #SDIO_ST_CMD_INHIBIT
  If needDat <> 0
    mask = mask | #SDIO_ST_DAT_INHIBIT
  EndIf
  budget = #SDIO_TMO_INHIBIT_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  While (sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS) & mask) <> 0
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_INHIBIT)
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_ResetLine - recover CMD (and optionally DAT) after an error, so
;  one bad command does not poison every command after it.
;
;  Software Reset For CMD Line and For DAT Line are self-clearing bits
;  in the Software Reset register at $2F (sdhci.h lines 130-133), which
;  is the top byte of the CONTROL1 word. They reset that part of the
;  controller WITHOUT disturbing the clock setup, which is why an error
;  path uses these two and not Software Reset For All.
;
;  Bounded, but its failure is not reported: it already runs inside a
;  failure path, and replacing the caller's error code with a second one
;  would hide the original problem.
; ======================================================================
Procedure sdio_ResetLine(alsoData.i)
  Define bits.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  bits = #SDIO_SRST_CMD
  If alsoData <> 0
    bits = bits | #SDIO_SRST_DATA
  EndIf
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL1, sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL1) | bits)
  budget = #SDIO_TMO_RESET_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  While (sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL1) & bits) <> 0
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      Break
    EndIf
  Wend
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_Command - issue one command and collect its response.
;
;  cmdtm     one of the #SDIO_CMDn words above, complete, including the
;            transfer-mode half for a data command
;  arg       the 32-bit argument
;  tmoIsOk   1 if a Command Timeout is an EXPECTED answer here. Two
;            callers need it: the CMD52 that resets the card before
;            anything is known to be there, and the first CMD5, which is
;            how this file discovers whether the slot is populated at
;            all. Shouting about either would turn a normal outcome into
;            a reported fault.
;
;  Returns  1  completed; sdio_resp0..3 are valid
;           0  failed; sdio_err says how
;          -1  timed out and tmoIsOk was set. sdio_err is NOT set - the
;              caller asked for this outcome. -1 rather than 0 so that a
;              caller which ignores the distinction still sees "not
;              success".
;
;  THE DATA PHASE IS NOT DONE HERE. A CMD53 leaves the buffer waiting;
;  sdio_Cmd53 below pumps it. Splitting them keeps this procedure the
;  same shape as emmc.pi4's sd_Command, which is a file somebody has
;  already read.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_Command(cmdtm.i, arg.i, tmoIsOk.i)
  Define intr.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define needDat.i
  Define rsp.i

  sdio_lastCmd = (cmdtm >> 24) & $3F

  needDat = 0
  rsp = cmdtm & #SDIO_CMD_RSP_MASK
  If rsp = #SDIO_CMD_RSP_48B
    needDat = 1
  EndIf
  If (cmdtm & #SDIO_CMD_ISDATA) <> 0
    needDat = 1
  EndIf

  If sdio_WaitInhibit(needDat) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Clear everything, so the poll below cannot see a stale bit from the
  ; previous command and call it success.
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, #SDIO_INT_ALL)

  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_ARG1, arg)
  ; ONE 32-BIT WRITE, transfer mode and command together. See rule 2 in
  ; the header: writing $0C alone would rewrite $0E and re-issue the
  ; previous command.
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CMDTM, cmdtm)

  budget = #SDIO_TMO_CMD_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  Repeat
    intr = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_INT_STATUS)
    If (intr & (#SDIO_INT_RESPONSE | #SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK)) <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      sdio_lastIntr = intr
      sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
      sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_CMD_STUCK
      sdio_ResetLine(1)
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  ForEver

  If (intr & #SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK) <> 0
    sdio_lastIntr = intr
    sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
    sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, intr)      ; write-1-to-clear what we saw
    sdio_ResetLine(1)
    ; A bare Command Timeout with nothing else set is the "no answer"
    ; case, and it is the one some callers expect.
    If (intr & #SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK) = #SDIO_INT_TIMEOUT
      If tmoIsOk <> 0
        ProcedureReturn -1
      EndIf
      sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_CMD_TIMEOUT
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_CMD_ERROR
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, #SDIO_INT_RESPONSE)

  sdio_resp0 = 0
  sdio_resp1 = 0
  sdio_resp2 = 0
  sdio_resp3 = 0
  If rsp <> #SDIO_CMD_RSP_NONE
    sdio_resp0 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_RESP0)
    If rsp = #SDIO_CMD_RSP_136
      sdio_resp1 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_RESP1)
      sdio_resp2 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_RESP2)
      sdio_resp3 = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_RESP3)
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; An R1b's busy period follows the response on DAT0 and ends with
  ; Transfer Complete, not with Command Complete. Waiting only for the
  ; latter lets the next command be issued into a card that is still
  ; busy. CMD7 is the only R1b this file sends and it is enough to make
  ; this necessary.
  If rsp = #SDIO_CMD_RSP_48B
    budget = #SDIO_TMO_DATA_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
    t0 = sdio_Ticks()
    Repeat
      intr = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_INT_STATUS)
      If (intr & #SDIO_INT_DATA_END) <> 0
        sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, #SDIO_INT_DATA_END)
        Break
      EndIf
      If (intr & #SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK) <> 0
        sdio_lastIntr = intr
        sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
        sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, intr)
        sdio_ResetLine(1)
        sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_CMD_ERROR
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
      ; Some controllers end an R1b busy by dropping the DAT inhibit and
      ; never raising Transfer Complete. Accept that rather than time
      ; out on a card that is, observably, no longer busy.
      If (sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS) & #SDIO_ST_DAT_INHIBIT) = 0
        Break
      EndIf
      If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
        sdio_lastIntr = intr
        sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
        sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_BUSY_TMO
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    ForEver
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  sdio_CheckR5 - did the card accept the command?
;
;  A CMD52 or CMD53 that the CONTROLLER completed without error can
;  still have been REFUSED by the card, and the refusal is in the
;  response, not in the interrupt register. sdio_ops.c lines 90-95 tests
;  three of the five flags one at a time to get distinct error numbers;
;  this tests all five at once, because a caller here gets one error
;  code and the raw R5 through SdioLastR5().
;
;  THIS IS THE CHECK THAT IS EASIEST TO LEAVE OUT AND WORST TO LEAVE
;  OUT. Without it a CMD52 to a function that does not exist returns
;  whatever garbage was in the data byte, and the layer above believes
;  it. Returns 1 if the card accepted it, 0 with sdio_err set otherwise.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i sdio_CheckR5()
  sdio_lastR5 = sdio_resp0 & $FFFF
  If (sdio_lastR5 & #SDIO_R5_BAD) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_R5)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - CMD52, single-byte register access.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioCmd52Read(fn, addr) - one byte out of function fn's register
;  space. Returns 0..255, or -1 with sdio_err set.
;
;  -1 AND NOT 0 FOR FAILURE, because 0 is a perfectly ordinary register
;  value and a caller that cannot tell them apart will happily decide a
;  chip is absent because a bus error looked like a zero. Every caller
;  in cyw43.pi4 tests for -1.
;
;  THE ARGUMENT IS BUILT EXACTLY AS sdio_ops.c LINE 76-80 BUILDS IT, and
;  note what a read does NOT set: no write bit, no RAW bit, and the data
;  byte is zero. Setting the RAW bit on a read is meaningless and some
;  cards treat the whole argument as malformed.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioCmd52Read(fn.i, addr.i)
  Define arg.i
  If fn < 0 Or fn > 7
    sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If addr < 0 Or addr > #SDIO_ADDR_MAX
    sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_ADDR)
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf

  arg = (fn * #SDIO_ARG_FN_STEP) | (addr * #SDIO_ARG_ADDR_STEP)
  If sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD52, arg, 0) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If sdio_CheckR5() = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn sdio_resp0 & $FF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioCmd52Write(fn, addr, val) - one byte in. Returns 1 or 0.
;
;  THE RAW FLAG IS SET. sdio_ops.c line 78 sets it when the caller asked
;  for the read-back value; this file always asks, because the byte the
;  card returns after a write is free evidence that the write landed,
;  and it is recorded in sdio_lastR5 whether anybody looks or not. It is
;  NOT compared against what was written: plenty of SDIO registers are
;  write-1-to-clear, or self-clearing, or read back a different field
;  entirely, and a driver that insisted on an echo would refuse half the
;  legitimate writes in cyw43.pi4.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioCmd52Write(fn.i, addr.i, val.i)
  Define arg.i
  If fn < 0 Or fn > 7
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC)
  EndIf
  If addr < 0 Or addr > #SDIO_ADDR_MAX
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_ADDR)
  EndIf

  arg = #SDIO_ARG_WRITE | #SDIO_ARG_RAW
  arg = arg | (fn * #SDIO_ARG_FN_STEP)
  arg = arg | (addr * #SDIO_ARG_ADDR_STEP)
  arg = arg | (val & $FF)
  If sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD52, arg, 0) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn sdio_CheckR5()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioCmd52ReadRaw(fn, addr) - the same read, but a command timeout is
;  not an error and returns -2.
;
;  For the one caller that legitimately expects no answer: the reset
;  CMD52 sent before anything is known to be on the bus.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioCmd52ReadRaw(fn.i, addr.i)
  Define arg.i
  Define r.i
  arg = (fn * #SDIO_ARG_FN_STEP) | (addr * #SDIO_ARG_ADDR_STEP)
  r = sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD52, arg, 1)
  If r = -1
    ProcedureReturn -2
  EndIf
  If r <> 1
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn sdio_resp0 & $FF
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - CMD53, multi-byte and multi-BLOCK transfer.
;
;  TWO MODES, ONE BODY. Both are whole 4-byte units, and that single
;  restriction is the only one left:
;
;    * BYTE MODE moves 4 to 512 bytes in one command. This is what every
;      backplane register access uses - a 32-bit read is a 4-byte byte-
;      mode CMD53 - and it is the mode that has run on silicon.
;
;    * BLOCK MODE moves 1 to 511 blocks of a size the CARD was told
;      beforehand with SdioSetBlockSize(). This is what moves a firmware
;      image or a WLAN frame, and it is why this section was reopened on
;      2026-08-26.
;
;  WHY THE 4-BYTE UNIT SURVIVES BOTH MODES. The Buffer Data Port is 32
;  bits wide. A trailing 1, 2 or 3 bytes would need a partial final
;  word, which means reading four bytes and discarding some - fine for a
;  read, WRONG for a write, where the discarded bytes are real bus
;  traffic to real registers. Rather than have the two directions behave
;  differently, both refuse. In practice nothing asks: F1's block size
;  is 64 and F2's is 512 (bcmsdh.c lines 44-45), and a backplane
;  register is four bytes by construction.
;
;  WHAT CHANGED, AND WHAT THE OLD COMMENT HERE SAID. Until today this
;  section carried a reasoned refusal of block mode whose third bullet
;  was "there is no firmware image here (see cyw43.pi4) and there are no
;  packets, so block mode would be untestable code in the path of every
;  transfer." That reasoning was sound and it has EXPIRED, not been
;  overruled: the image is on the disk now, so block mode is testable,
;  and the argument that kept it out is the argument that now requires
;  it. The two premises it turned on are both recorded above so that a
;  reader meeting this code cold can see it was a decision and not an
;  oversight.
;
;  BLOCK MODE IS NOT IN THE PATH OF EVERY TRANSFER even so. The byte-
;  mode entry points still take the identical path they took when they
;  were proven, with blocks = 1 - the loop below simply runs once. That
;  was a deliberate constraint on this change: no new code executes on
;  the path that already works.
;
;  A caller asking for something outside the contract gets
;  #SDIO_ERR_BAD_LEN, #SDIO_ERR_BAD_COUNT or #SDIO_ERR_ALIGN. It does
;  not get a silently truncated transfer.
;
; ======================================================================
;  512 IS THE PROTOCOL'S BYTE-MODE CEILING. IT IS NOT THE CARD'S.
;  READ THIS BEFORE ASKING FOR A LONG BYTE-MODE TRANSFER.
; ======================================================================
;  This file will accept any byte-mode length up to 512 because that is
;  all the nine-bit count field can express. THE CARD IS ENTITLED TO
;  REFUSE A SHORTER ONE, and on 2026-08-26 this board's CYW43455 did:
;  a 256-byte byte-mode read of function 1 came back with an R5 refusal.
;
;  Linux never issues one, and the reason is sdio_max_byte_size() at
;  v6.12_linux_mmc_sdio_io.c lines 189-202:
;
;      unsigned mval = func->card->host->max_blk_size;
;      if (mmc_blksz_for_byte_mode(func->card))
;              mval = min(mval, func->cur_blksize);
;      else
;              mval = min(mval, func->max_blksize);
;      ...
;      return min(mval, 512u);   /* maximum size for byte mode */
;
;  Three separate bounds, and the smallest wins. On this board the host
;  bound is 512 (bcm2835-mmc.c line 1360, "mmc->max_blk_size = 512"),
;  and the FUNCTION bound is the one that bites: function 1's block size
;  is 64 (bcmsdh.c line 44), so a byte-mode transfer to function 1 is at
;  most 64 bytes however much the argument field could hold.
;
;  WHY THIS FILE DOES NOT ENFORCE IT. It does not know the block size -
;  SdioSetBlockSize writes it and nothing reads it back, and the CIS
;  that carries a function's MAXIMUM block size is not parsed here (see
;  "NO CIS PARSING" in the header). Enforcing a bound this file cannot
;  measure would mean inventing one. So the bound is DOCUMENTED here and
;  APPLIED by the caller that does know: cyw43.pi4 reads function 1's
;  block size back out of the card in Cyw43Attach and chunks its
;  byte-mode transfers to it.
;
;  THE CONSERVATIVE BRANCH IS THE ONE TO TAKE when the maximum is
;  unknown. min(host, cur_blksize) is the mmc_blksz_for_byte_mode branch
;  and it is provably no larger than the other branch, because a
;  function's current block size can never exceed its maximum. A caller
;  that uses it cannot ask for too much; it can only ask for too little,
;  and too little costs speed on a path that exists to be slow and
;  obviously correct.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_Cmd53Xfer - the common body for both modes.
;
;    dir        1 = read from the card, 0 = write to it
;    blksz      bytes per block. In byte mode this IS the whole
;               transfer length.
;    blocks     how many. Must be 1 in byte mode.
;    blockMode  0 = byte mode, 1 = block mode
;    incr       1 walks the card's address, 0 hammers one address
;
;  THE BLOCK SIZE / BLOCK COUNT REGISTER. BLOCK_SIZE is 16 bits at $04
;  and BLOCK_COUNT is 16 bits at $06 (sdhci.h lines 30 and 33), so the
;  word is (count << 16) | size. In byte mode the whole transfer is one
;  "block" of nbytes and the count is 1; in block mode both fields mean
;  what they say.
;
;  THE DMA BOUNDARY FIELD AT BITS 14:12 IS LEFT AT ZERO IN BOTH MODES,
;  and the difference from Linux is deliberate. SDHCI_MAKE_BLKSZ
;  (sdhci.h line 31) puts a 3-bit "host SDMA buffer boundary" there and
;  bcm2835-mmc.c line 607 passes SDHCI_DEFAULT_BOUNDARY_ARG, which
;  sdhci.h lines 301-302 compute as ilog2(512 * 1024) - 12 = 7. That
;  field tells the controller when to raise a DMA Interrupt so the
;  driver can reprogram the system address mid-transfer. THIS FILE IS
;  PIO ONLY - SDHCI_TRNS_DMA is never set and SDHCI_DMA_ADDRESS is never
;  written - so the field selects the boundary of a DMA that does not
;  happen. Writing 7 would be copying a value for its own sake; writing
;  0 keeps one rule for both modes and leaves the byte-mode path
;  bit-identical to the one already proven on the board.
;
;  BLOCK COUNT ENABLE IS SET even for one block, because bcm2835-mmc.c
;  line 630 sets it unconditionally whenever a command carries data:
;      mode = SDHCI_TRNS_BLK_CNT_EN;
;  It is folded into the four #SDIO_CMD53_* constants rather than being
;  written separately, because transfer mode and command must go out as
;  one 32-bit store.
;
;  THE ARGUMENT'S LENGTH FIELD, sdio_ops.c lines 141-142:
;      if (blocks == 0) cmd.arg |= (blksz == 512) ? 0 : blksz;
;      else             cmd.arg |= 0x08000000 | blocks;
;  Byte mode encodes the BYTE count in nine bits, and 512 does not fit
;  in nine bits, so 512 is spelled 0. This file does not special-case
;  that, because it does not have to: 512 & $1FF is already 0. The
;  masking is written out anyway rather than left implicit, so that the
;  next person to read the line does not have to rediscover the
;  coincidence.
;
;  THE PER-BLOCK WAIT. Buffer Read Ready and Buffer Write Ready are
;  raised ONCE PER BLOCK by an SDHCI controller, not once per transfer,
;  and each is cleared by writing it back. So the wait sits inside the
;  block loop and the flag is tested once per block rather than once per
;  word: the interrupt means a WHOLE block's worth of the FIFO is
;  available. bcm2835-mmc.c reaches the same place from the other
;  direction - its transfer_pio (line 454) loops on the PRESENT_STATE
;  bit and decrements host->blocks once per pass.
;
;  EVERY BLOCK GETS THE FULL #SDIO_TMO_DATA_MS BUDGET, not a share of
;  one budget for the whole transfer. A 511-block transfer is not 511
;  times more likely to stall in any one place, and a budget divided by
;  the block count would make a large transfer fail for being large.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure sdio_Cmd53Done(t0.i, ok.i)
  Define dt.i
  dt = sdio_Ticks() - t0
  sdio_cmd53Ticks = sdio_cmd53Ticks + dt
  If dt > sdio_cmd53MaxTicks
    sdio_cmd53MaxTicks = dt
  EndIf
  If ok = 0
    sdio_cmd53Failures = sdio_cmd53Failures + 1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i sdio_Cmd53Xfer(dir.i, fn.i, addr.i, *buf, blksz.i, blocks.i, blockMode.i, incr.i)
  Define arg.i
  Define cmdtm.i
  Define words.i
  Define i.i
  Define b.i
  Define intr.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define want.i
  Define p.i
  Define xferT0.i
  Define waitTicks.i

  If *buf = 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NULL_BUF)
  EndIf
  If (*buf & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_ALIGN)
  EndIf
  If fn < 0 Or fn > 7
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC)
  EndIf
  If addr < 0 Or addr > #SDIO_ADDR_MAX
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_ADDR)
  EndIf
  If blksz < 4 Or blksz > 512 Or (blksz & 3) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  If blocks < 1 Or blocks > #SDIO_BLOCKS_MAX
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_COUNT)
  EndIf
  ; Byte mode has no block count to carry. Silently accepting blocks = 3
  ; and moving one block would be the exact shape of quiet wrong answer
  ; this file exists to refuse, so the contradiction is refused instead.
  If blockMode = 0 And blocks <> 1
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_COUNT)
  EndIf

  xferT0 = sdio_Ticks()
  sdio_cmd53Calls = sdio_cmd53Calls + 1
  sdio_cmd53Bytes = sdio_cmd53Bytes + (blksz * blocks)
  If dir = 1
    sdio_cmd53ReadCalls = sdio_cmd53ReadCalls + 1
  Else
    sdio_cmd53WriteCalls = sdio_cmd53WriteCalls + 1
  EndIf
  If blockMode <> 0
    sdio_cmd53BlockCalls = sdio_cmd53BlockCalls + 1
  Else
    sdio_cmd53ByteCalls = sdio_cmd53ByteCalls + 1
  EndIf

  arg = 0
  If dir = 0
    arg = #SDIO_ARG_WRITE
  EndIf
  arg = arg | (fn * #SDIO_ARG_FN_STEP)
  If incr <> 0
    arg = arg | #SDIO_ARG_INCR
  EndIf
  arg = arg | (addr * #SDIO_ARG_ADDR_STEP)
  If blockMode <> 0
    arg = arg | #SDIO_ARG_BLOCKMODE
    arg = arg | (blocks & $1FF)
  Else
    arg = arg | (blksz & $1FF)
  EndIf

  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_BLKSIZECNT, (blocks << 16) | (blksz & $FFF))

  If dir = 1
    If blocks > 1
      cmdtm = #SDIO_CMD53_READ_MULTI
    Else
      cmdtm = #SDIO_CMD53_READ
    EndIf
  Else
    If blocks > 1
      cmdtm = #SDIO_CMD53_WRITE_MULTI
    Else
      cmdtm = #SDIO_CMD53_WRITE
    EndIf
  EndIf

  If sdio_Command(cmdtm, arg, 0) <> 1
    ; No reset here: sdio_Command resets the line itself on every path
    ; that can leave the controller mid-transfer - the error-interrupt
    ; branch and the command-stuck branch both call sdio_ResetLine(1)
    ; before returning. Adding a second reset would be harmless and
    ; would also be a claim that the first one is not trusted.
    sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 0)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ====================================================================
  ;  THE CARD REFUSED THE COMMAND, AND THE DATA LINE MUST BE RESET.
  ;
  ;  FOUND ON SILICON 2026-08-26. This is the one failure the controller
  ;  does not consider a failure: Command Complete is raised, no error
  ;  bit is set anywhere, the response is perfectly well formed - and
  ;  what it SAYS is that the card is not going to do it. The data phase
  ;  the controller has been told to expect will therefore never happen,
  ;  so DAT Inhibit stays asserted for ever, and sdio_WaitInhibit at the
  ;  top of the NEXT data command spins until its budget runs out.
  ;
  ;  WHAT THAT LOOKS LIKE ON A BENCH, because it is deeply misleading:
  ;  one command fails with #SDIO_ERR_R5, and then EVERY command after
  ;  it fails with #SDIO_ERR_INHIBIT and an interrupt status of
  ;  $00000000 - no bits set at all, because nothing ever started. The
  ;  second symptom is louder, arrives more often, and has nothing to do
  ;  with the actual fault. A bench log on 2026-08-26 showed exactly
  ;  that: a refused 256-byte byte-mode read, then an inhibit timeout on
  ;  the very next transfer, then "a 32-bit backplane read failed" three
  ;  procedures away in cyw43.pi4, all from this one missing line.
  ;
  ;  THIS BUG PREDATES BLOCK MODE. The R5 check has returned without a
  ;  reset since this file was written; it had simply never fired,
  ;  because until today every CMD53 in this project was a four-byte
  ;  backplane register access that no card would refuse. The block-mode
  ;  work did not introduce it, it produced the first command the card
  ;  was willing to say no to.
  ; ====================================================================
  If sdio_CheckR5() = 0
    sdio_ResetLine(1)
    sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 0)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- the data phase -------------------------------------------------
  If dir = 1
    want = #SDIO_INT_DATA_AVAIL
  Else
    want = #SDIO_INT_SPACE_AVAIL
  EndIf

  words = blksz / 4
  p = *buf

  For b = 1 To blocks
    budget = #SDIO_TMO_DATA_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
    t0 = sdio_Ticks()
    Repeat
      intr = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_INT_STATUS)
      If (intr & want) <> 0
        sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, want)
        waitTicks = sdio_Ticks() - t0
        sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks = sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks + waitTicks
        If waitTicks > sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks
          sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks = waitTicks
        EndIf
        Break
      EndIf
      If (intr & #SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK) <> 0
        sdio_lastXferBlock = b
        sdio_lastIntr = intr
        sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
        sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, intr)
        sdio_ResetLine(1)
        sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_DATA_ERROR
        waitTicks = sdio_Ticks() - t0
        sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks = sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks + waitTicks
        If waitTicks > sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks
          sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks = waitTicks
        EndIf
        sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 0)
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
      If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
        sdio_lastXferBlock = b
        sdio_lastIntr = intr
        sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
        sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_DATA_TMO
        waitTicks = sdio_Ticks() - t0
        sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks = sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks + waitTicks
        If waitTicks > sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks
          sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks = waitTicks
        EndIf
        sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 0)
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    ForEver

    If dir = 1
      For i = 0 To words - 1
        PokeL(p, sdio_Rd(#SDIO_BUFFER))
        p = p + 4
      Next
    Else
      For i = 0 To words - 1
        sdio_Wr(#SDIO_BUFFER, PeekN(p))
        p = p + 4
      Next
    EndIf
  Next

  ; --- Transfer Complete ----------------------------------------------
  ; ONE Transfer Complete for the WHOLE command, however many blocks it
  ; carried. The controller raises it when the last block's CRC status
  ; has been seen, which is why it is waited for out here and not inside
  ; the loop.
  budget = #SDIO_TMO_DATA_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  Repeat
    intr = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_INT_STATUS)
    If (intr & #SDIO_INT_DATA_END) <> 0
      sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, #SDIO_INT_DATA_END)
      waitTicks = sdio_Ticks() - t0
      sdio_cmd53EndTicks = sdio_cmd53EndTicks + waitTicks
      If waitTicks > sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks
        sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks = waitTicks
      EndIf
      Break
    EndIf
    If (intr & #SDIO_INT_ERROR_MASK) <> 0
      sdio_lastXferBlock = blocks
      sdio_lastIntr = intr
      sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
      sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, intr)
      sdio_ResetLine(1)
      sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_DATA_ERROR
      waitTicks = sdio_Ticks() - t0
      sdio_cmd53EndTicks = sdio_cmd53EndTicks + waitTicks
      If waitTicks > sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks
        sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks = waitTicks
      EndIf
      sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 0)
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      sdio_lastXferBlock = blocks
      sdio_lastIntr = intr
      sdio_lastStat = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_STATUS)
      sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_XFER_TMO
      waitTicks = sdio_Ticks() - t0
      sdio_cmd53EndTicks = sdio_cmd53EndTicks + waitTicks
      If waitTicks > sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks
        sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks = waitTicks
      EndIf
      sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 0)
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  ForEver

  sdio_Cmd53Done(xferT0, 1)
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_Cmd53 - the byte-mode form, kept as its own name because two
;  call sites and one bench log already use it. One block of nbytes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i sdio_Cmd53(dir.i, fn.i, addr.i, *buf, nbytes.i, incr.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_Cmd53Xfer(dir, fn, addr, *buf, nbytes, 1, 0, incr)
EndProcedure

; incr = 1 walks the card's address as it goes; incr = 0 hammers one
; address, which is how a FIFO is drained. See SDIOH_DATA_FIX /
; SDIOH_DATA_INC, Datasheets/pi4/brcmfmac/v6.12_brcmfmac_sdio.h lines
; 133-134.
Procedure.i SdioCmd53Read(fn.i, addr.i, *buf, nbytes.i, incr.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_Cmd53(1, fn, addr, *buf, nbytes, incr)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioCmd53Write(fn.i, addr.i, *buf, nbytes.i, incr.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_Cmd53(0, fn, addr, *buf, nbytes, incr)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PUBLIC - the block-mode pair.
;
;  THE CARD MUST ALREADY AGREE ABOUT blkSize. It is not carried in the
;  command; it comes from the function's FBR block-size register, which
;  SdioSetBlockSize() writes. Pass a blkSize here that differs from the
;  one the card holds and the transfer does not fail cleanly - the
;  controller and the card simply count different numbers of bytes, and
;  what comes back is misaligned data with no error bit anywhere. That
;  is the single most dangerous mistake available in this file, which is
;  why it is stated here and not left to the caller to infer.
;
;  Total bytes moved is blkSize * blocks. With F1's 64 that is at most
;  32,704 per call and with F2's 512 at most 261,632; a 609 KB firmware
;  image is therefore about nineteen calls at F1's block size, and the
;  windowing loop above this one in cyw43.pi4 will cut it finer than
;  that anyway because the backplane window is only 32 KB.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioCmd53ReadBlocks(fn.i, addr.i, *buf, blkSize.i, blocks.i, incr.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_Cmd53Xfer(1, fn, addr, *buf, blkSize, blocks, 1, incr)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioCmd53WriteBlocks(fn.i, addr.i, *buf, blkSize.i, blocks.i, incr.i)
  ProcedureReturn sdio_Cmd53Xfer(0, fn, addr, *buf, blkSize, blocks, 1, incr)
EndProcedure

; Which block was in flight when the last block-mode transfer failed,
; counted from 1. Zero means none has. See sdio_lastXferBlock.
Procedure.i SdioLastXferBlock()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_lastXferBlock
EndProcedure

; The card-side ceiling on a single block-mode command's block count.
; Exposed so a caller can size its own chunking loop from the library
; rather than from a number typed into two files.
Procedure.i SdioMaxBlocks()
  ProcedureReturn #SDIO_BLOCKS_MAX
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - function enable, block size, bus width.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioEnableFunction(fn) - turn an I/O function on and wait for it to
;  say it is ready.
;
;  sdio_io.c lines 70-91, sdio_enable_func():
;      read CCCR_IOEx, OR in (1 << fn), write it back;
;      then poll CCCR_IORx until (1 << fn) is set, or time out.
;
;  READ-MODIFY-WRITE, NOT A SLAM, and the reason matters here more than
;  usual: IOEx holds one bit per function, so writing a bare (1 << fn)
;  DISABLES every other function. On a CYW43455 that means enabling the
;  data function turns the backplane function off underneath the driver
;  that is using it.
;
;  THE POLL IS NOT OPTIONAL EITHER. IOEx is a request; IORx is the
;  answer. The part takes real time to bring a function up, and a driver
;  that writes IOEx and carries straight on gets a run of failed CMD52s
;  that look like a wiring fault.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioEnableFunction(fn.i)
  Define reg.i
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i

  If fn < 1 Or fn > 7
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC)
  EndIf

  reg = SdioCmd52Read(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IOEx)
  If reg < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If SdioCmd52Write(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IOEx, reg | (1 << fn)) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  budget = #SDIO_TMO_ENABLE_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  Repeat
    reg = SdioCmd52Read(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IORx)
    If reg < 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If (reg & (1 << fn)) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_ENABLE_TMO)
    EndIf
  ForEver
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioDisableFunction(fn) - the same read-modify-write, inverted. No
;  poll: IORx clearing is not something the specification promises a
;  deadline for, and a driver that hangs waiting to turn something OFF
;  has made a bad situation worse.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioDisableFunction(fn.i)
  Define reg.i
  If fn < 1 Or fn > 7
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC)
  EndIf
  reg = SdioCmd52Read(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IOEx)
  If reg < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn SdioCmd52Write(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IOEx, reg & (~(1 << fn)))
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioSetBlockSize(fn, bytes) - the function's FBR block size.
;
;  SDIO_FBR_BASE(f) is f * $100 (sdio.h line 171) and SDIO_FBR_BLKSIZE
;  is $10 within it (line 192), two bytes, low byte first.
;
;  This file never uses block mode, so this changes nothing about how
;  IT talks to the card. It is here because the CYW43455's firmware
;  reads these registers to decide its own framing, and cyw43.pi4 has to
;  be able to set them - see brcmf_sdiod_probe, which sets 64 bytes on
;  both functions before doing anything else.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioSetBlockSize(fn.i, bytes.i)
  Define base.i
  If fn < 0 Or fn > 7
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_FUNC)
  EndIf
  If bytes < 1 Or bytes > 2048
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_LEN)
  EndIf
  base = fn * #SDIO_FBR_STEP + #SDIO_FBR_BLKSIZE
  If SdioCmd52Write(0, base, bytes & $FF) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn SdioCmd52Write(0, base + 1, (bytes >> 8) & $FF)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  sdio_SetBusWidth4 - four data lines, card first then controller.
;
;  core/sdio.c lines 329-341, sdio_enable_4bit_bus(): read CCCR_IF,
;  clear the width field, set SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_4BIT, write it back. Then
;  the HOST is told, which is the order that matters: tell the
;  controller first and it starts driving four lines at a card that is
;  still listening on one.
;
;  THE CARD-DETECT DISABLE BIT IS ALSO SET. SDIO_BUS_CD_DISABLE ($80,
;  sdio.h line 106) turns off the card's pull-up on DAT3. In one-bit
;  mode DAT3 is the card-detect resistor; in four-bit mode it is a data
;  line, and leaving a pull-up on a driven data line is exactly the
;  thing uart.pi4's header complains about on a driven TX pin. Linux
;  does the same at core/sdio.c line 337.
;
;  A FAILURE HERE IS NOT FATAL TO THE CALLER, and that is deliberate:
;  the bus works at one bit, just slower. SdioInit reports it through
;  SdioBusWidth() rather than refusing, because a radio that talks
;  slowly is worth a great deal more than one that does not talk.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i sdio_SetBusWidth4()
  Define ctrl.i
  Define hc.i

  ctrl = SdioCmd52Read(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IF)
  If ctrl < 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ctrl = ctrl & (~#SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_MASK)
  ctrl = ctrl | #SDIO_BUS_WIDTH_4BIT | #SDIO_BUS_CD_DISABLE
  If SdioCmd52Write(0, #SDIO_CCCR_IF, ctrl) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Host Control is byte 0 of the word at $28.
  hc = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL0)
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL0, hc | #SDIO_HC_4BITBUS)
  sdio_width4 = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - configuration a caller supplies.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioSetBaseClockKhz(khz) - hand this file the base clock the
;  Capabilities register does not carry. See THE BASE CLOCK.
;
;  ASK THE MAILBOX FOR CLOCK ID 1 (EMMC), NOT 12 (EMMC2). Those are
;  different clocks on this chip and only one of them feeds this
;  controller. Pasting emmc.pi4's number in here is the mistake this
;  paragraph exists to stop.
;
;      MailboxInit()
;      SdioSetBaseClockKhz(MailboxClockRate(1) / 1000)
;
;  Zero or negative clears it and puts the Capabilities register back in
;  charge, which means SdioInit() will refuse again if it says nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure SdioSetBaseClockKhz(khz.i)
  If khz > 0
    sdio_userBase = khz
  Else
    sdio_userBase = 0
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SdioInit - the whole bring-up.
;
;  THE ORDER IS THE SDIO SPECIFICATION'S, by way of
;  v6.12_linux_mmc_core_sdio.c. Its own comment at lines 633-646 is
;  worth having here, because it explains a step that looks redundant:
;
;      "sdio_reset() is technically not needed. Having just powered up
;       the card ... The CMD5 reset (mmc_send_io_op_cond()), according
;       to the SDIO spec ..."
;
;  and it does it anyway, because a card that was left running by an
;  earlier boot has not just powered up. On a bare-metal image loaded by
;  U-Boot on a board that has been on for a while, that is the NORMAL
;  case, not the exception - so the reset stays.
;
;      1. CNTFRQ sanity. Refuse if the clock is lying: every "bounded"
;         wait below is unbounded without it.
;      1a. THE FIRMWARE HALF, added 2026-08-26 and all of it advisory:
;         the EMMC clock and power device 0 turned on if the firmware
;         says they exist and are off, then WL_ON power-cycled and
;         driven high. None of it can fail this procedure - see rule 2
;         at THE FIRMWARE HALF OF THE BRING-UP - but all of it happens
;         BEFORE the pins are muxed and the controller is inspected,
;         because a radio that is coming up needs its 150 ms and the
;         register work below is the natural place to spend it.
;      2. Mux the pins.
;      3. Is there a controller here at all? VERSION ONLY. Capabilities
;         is recorded, not judged; see sdio_ControllerPlausible.
;      4. Base clock, or refuse.
;      5. Software Reset For All.
;      6. Power the bus at 3.3V.
;      7. Clock at 400 kHz.
;      8. Unmask the status bits (but not the card interrupt).
;      9. CMD52 abort/reset - a timeout here is EXPECTED and ignored.
;     10. CMD0 GO_IDLE_STATE.
;     11. CMD5 with argument 0 - the probe. A timeout means no card.
;     12. CMD5 with the voltage window, until the card says ready.
;     13. CMD3 - the card publishes an RCA.
;     14. CMD7 - select it.
;     15. Clock up to 25 MHz.
;     16. Four-bit bus, if the card will.
;
;  WHAT IS NOT HERE: no function is enabled and no block size is set.
;  Both are the DEVICE's business, not the BUS's, and cyw43.pi4 does
;  them. A generic SDIO bring-up that turned function 1 on would be
;  making an assumption about what is plugged in.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with sdio_err set.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i SdioInit()
  Define t0.i
  Define budget.i
  Define r.i
  Define v.i

  sdio_ready = 0
  sdio_err   = #SDIO_ERR_NONE
  sdio_width4 = 0
  sdio_rca   = 0
  sdio_ocr   = 0
  sdio_nfunc = 0
  sdio_cmd53Calls = 0
  sdio_cmd53ReadCalls = 0
  sdio_cmd53WriteCalls = 0
  sdio_cmd53Bytes = 0
  sdio_cmd53BlockCalls = 0
  sdio_cmd53ByteCalls = 0
  sdio_cmd53Failures = 0
  sdio_cmd53Ticks = 0
  sdio_cmd53MaxTicks = 0
  sdio_cmd53ReadyTicks = 0
  sdio_cmd53ReadyMaxTicks = 0
  sdio_cmd53EndTicks = 0
  sdio_cmd53EndMaxTicks = 0

  ; --- 1 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  sdio_hz = sdio_TickHz()
  If sdio_hz <= 0 Or sdio_hz > #SDIO_HZ_MAX
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ)
  EndIf
  sdio_tickPerMs = sdio_hz / 1000
  If sdio_tickPerMs < 1
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NO_CNTFRQ)
  EndIf

  ; --- 1a --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Advisory, every line of it. If mailbox.pi4 was never initialised
  ; these three calls do nothing at all and leave their accessors saying
  ; "not attempted"; if the firmware refuses a tag they record the
  ; refusal and carry on. Nothing below depends on any of it succeeding,
  ; and that is deliberate: this file's subject is the controller.
  sdio_EnableClockAndPower()
  SdioAssertWlOn()

  ; --- 2 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  sdio_MuxPins()

  ; --- 3 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  If sdio_ControllerPlausible() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 4 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  sdio_baseKhz = sdio_BaseClockKhz()
  If sdio_baseKhz <= 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BASECLK)
  EndIf

  ; --- 5 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Software Reset For All clears the clock setup as well, which is why
  ; the clock is programmed AFTER it and not before.
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL1, #SDIO_SRST_ALL)
  budget = #SDIO_TMO_RESET_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  While (sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL1) & #SDIO_SRST_ALL) <> 0
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_RESET)
    EndIf
  Wend

  ; --- 6 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Power Control is byte 1 of the word at $28. Set the voltage FIRST
  ; and the on bit in the same write - the specification allows one
  ; write, and staging it in two leaves a moment at an undefined
  ; voltage.
  v = sdio_Rd(#SDIO_CONTROL0) & (~#SDIO_PWR_MASK)
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_CONTROL0, v | #SDIO_PWR_330 | #SDIO_PWR_ON)
  ; The rail has to settle. There is no status bit for this; the SD
  ; specification's own figure is a millisecond, and a millisecond spent
  ; here once is not worth optimising.
  sdio_DelayUs(1000)

  ; --- 7 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  If sdio_SetClock(#SDIO_CLK_IDENT_KHZ) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 8 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Status bits on, SIGNAL enable off. Nothing here uses interrupts; the
  ; distinction is that INT_ENABLE controls what appears in
  ; INT_STATUS at all, while SIGNAL_ENABLE controls what reaches the
  ; interrupt controller. Leaving INT_ENABLE clear is a classic way to
  ; write a polling driver that polls a register that never changes.
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_ENABLE, #SDIO_INT_ENABLE_SET)
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_SIG_ENABLE, 0)
  sdio_Wr(#SDIO_INT_STATUS, #SDIO_INT_ALL)

  ; --- 9 ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ; sdio_ops.c lines 209-215. The read is allowed to fail; if it does,
  ; a bare $08 is written, which is what that function does too.
  r = SdioCmd52ReadRaw(0, #SDIO_CCCR_ABORT)
  If r < 0
    r = 0
  EndIf
  ; Built in a variable, not inline: a call argument list has to fit on
  ; one line on this parser, and this one does not.
  v = #SDIO_ARG_WRITE | (#SDIO_CCCR_ABORT * #SDIO_ARG_ADDR_STEP)
  v = v | (r | #SDIO_ABORT_RES)
  sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD52, v, 1)

  ; --- 10 --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; CMD0 has no response, so there is nothing to check beyond the
  ; controller accepting it. A failure here is the CONTROLLER, not the
  ; card - the card cannot refuse a command it does not answer.
  If sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD0, 0, 0) <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 11 --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; The probe. sdio_ops.c line 33: "if we're just probing, do a single
  ; pass". A timeout is the answer "nothing is there", not a fault.
  r = sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD5, 0, 1)
  If r = -1
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NO_CARD)
  EndIf
  If r <> 1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  sdio_ocr   = sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_OCR_MASK
  sdio_nfunc = (sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_NFUNC) >> 28
  sdio_mem   = 0
  If (sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_MEMORY) <> 0
    sdio_mem = 1
  EndIf
  sdio_18v = 0
  If (sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_18V) <> 0
    sdio_18v = 1
  EndIf

  ; core/sdio.c line 1269 reads the same field and line 1275 iterates
  ; over it. Zero I/O functions means whatever answered is not an SDIO
  ; device, and pressing on would enumerate nothing while reporting
  ; success.
  If sdio_nfunc < 1
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NO_IO_FUNCS)
  EndIf

  ; --- 12 --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Ask for the intersection of what the card offers and what this board
  ; supplies. Asking for a window the card does not support is how a
  ; card is told to go permanently inactive, which needs a power cycle
  ; to undo - so the AND is not a nicety.
  v = sdio_ocr & #SDIO_OCR_32_34
  If v = 0
    ; The card published an OCR with nothing in the 3.2-3.4V range. On
    ; this board that means a 1.8V-only part, and there is no way from
    ; here to switch it - see "NO HIGH SPEED, NO UHS, NO 1.8V SWITCH".
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_NO_CARD)
  EndIf

  budget = #SDIO_TMO_OPCOND_MS * sdio_tickPerMs
  t0 = sdio_Ticks()
  Repeat
    If sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD5, v, 0) <> 1
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If (sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_READY) <> 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If (sdio_Ticks() - t0) > budget
      sdio_ocr = sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_OCR_MASK
      ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_OPCOND_TMO)
    EndIf
    ; sdio_ops.c line 53 waits 10 ms between tries. Hammering CMD5 at
    ; bus speed is not faster; it is the same wait with more traffic.
    sdio_DelayUs(10000)
  ForEver
  sdio_ocr = sdio_resp0 & #SDIO_R4_OCR_MASK

  ; --- 13 --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; sd_ops.c lines 267-283: "*rca = cmd.resp[0] >> 16".
  If sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD3, 0, 0) <> 1
    sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_CMD3
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  sdio_rca = (sdio_resp0 >> 16) & $FFFF
  ; RCA 0 is the broadcast address. A card that publishes it cannot be
  ; selected, and CMD7 with argument 0 DESELECTS everything - so this
  ; would look like a working select and leave the bus idle.
  If sdio_rca = 0
    ProcedureReturn sdio_Fail(#SDIO_ERR_BAD_RCA)
  EndIf

  ; --- 14 --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; CMD7's argument is the RCA in bits 31:16 (v6.12_linux_mmc_mmc.h line
  ; 38: "ac [31:16] RCA").
  If sdio_Command(#SDIO_CMD7, sdio_rca << 16, 0) <> 1
    sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_CMD7
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 15 --------------------------------------------------------------
  If sdio_SetClock(#SDIO_CLK_XFER_KHZ) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; --- 16 --------------------------------------------------------------
  ; Not fatal - see sdio_SetBusWidth4's note. The error code is cleared
  ; afterwards so a caller does not read a stale failure out of
  ; SdioLastError() on a successful init.
  If sdio_SetBusWidth4() = 0
    sdio_err = #SDIO_ERR_NONE
  EndIf

  sdio_ready = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  PUBLIC - what happened. All read-only.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i SdioReady()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_ready
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioLastError()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_err
EndProcedure

; The command INDEX of the last command issued, or -1 if none.
Procedure.i SdioLastCmd()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_lastCmd
EndProcedure

; The raw Interrupt Status word as it stood when something went wrong.
Procedure.i SdioLastIntr()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_lastIntr
EndProcedure

; The raw Present State word, likewise.
Procedure.i SdioLastStatus()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_lastStat
EndProcedure

; The low sixteen bits of the last R5 response - the card's own verdict
; on the last CMD52 or CMD53. Distinct from SdioLastIntr(), which is the
; CONTROLLER's verdict, and the two disagree more often than anyone
; expects.
Procedure.i SdioLastR5()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_lastR5
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioHostVersion()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_ver
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioHostVendor()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_vendor
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioCaps0()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_caps0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioCaps1()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_caps1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The evidence a reader needs in order to disbelieve this file.
;
;  These exist because of one bench day. The diagnostic printed an error
;  string and four status words, and NONE of the four was the register
;  the error was actually about, so the two numbers that mattered -
;  $FE3000FC and $FE300040 - had to be read out of a monitor prompt by
;  hand before anybody could tell what had happened. Anything a refusal
;  is based on should be printable without a second tool.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; The whole word at $FC: Slot Interrupt Status in the low half, Host
; Controller Version in the high half. On this board, $99020000.
Procedure.i SdioHostVersionRaw()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_hostVerRaw
EndProcedure

; 1 when the Capabilities register read all-zero or all-ones. THIS IS
; NOT AN ERROR - it was until 2026-08-26 and the board said otherwise.
; See THE CAPABILITIES REGISTER READS ZERO in the header.
Procedure.i SdioCapsAreZero()
  If sdio_caps0 = 0 Or sdio_caps0 = $FFFFFFFF
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; 1 if any firmware property tag was sent by this file at all. 0 means
; the mailbox was never ready, so every state accessor below is -1
; because it was never asked, not because the answer was no.
Procedure.i SdioMailboxUsed()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_mbxUsed
EndProcedure

; GET_CLOCK_STATE for clock 1 (EMMC), as found and after any set. Bit 0
; on/off, bit 1 "does not exist". -1 means not asked.
Procedure.i SdioClockStateBefore()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_clkState
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioClockStateAfter()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_clkStateAfter
EndProcedure

; GET_POWER_STATE for device 0, same encoding, same -1.
Procedure.i SdioPowerStateBefore()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_pwrState
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioPowerStateAfter()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_pwrStateAfter
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioWlOnStep - how far SdioAssertWlOn() got, and SdioWlOnStepText -
;  the same thing as an English sentence.
;
;  THE SENTENCES LIVE IN THIS FILE ON PURPOSE. The bench asked for the
;  ladder to be printed by the program rather than looked up in a
;  report, and that is right: the person reading a serial log has the
;  log and nothing else. Every rung below is ONE event - the previous
;  version folded "the message would not lay out" together with "the
;  send did not complete" into a single number, and the board landed on
;  exactly that number, which cost a round trip to disambiguate.
;
;  Pair it with SdioWlOnMailboxError(), which is MailboxError() captured
;  at whichever rung stopped: #MBX_ERR_TAG (8) means the FIRMWARE
;  declined the tag, #MBX_ERR_TXTIMEOUT (4) or #MBX_ERR_RXTIMEOUT (5)
;  mean the TRANSPORT did not complete, and those lead different places.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioWlOnStep()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnStep
EndProcedure

; The rung, as a full sentence. The caller prints it; nothing here
; assumes a particular output library.
Procedure.i SdioWlOnStepText()
  Select sdio_wlOnStep
    Case 0
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: the mailbox was never ready, so nothing at all was sent - call MailboxInit() first"
    Case 1
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: the GET_GPIO_CONFIG message could not be laid out in the mailbox buffer - that is this side of the link, not the firmware"
    Case 2
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: GET_GPIO_CONFIG was sent and the transaction did not complete - read the mailbox error printed beside this line"
    Case 3
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: GET_GPIO_CONFIG completed but the firmware echoed the pin number back instead of clearing it, which is how it rejects a pin - this firmware does not accept GPIO 129"
    Case 4
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: the GET_GPIO_STATE message could not be laid out in the mailbox buffer"
    Case 5
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: GET_GPIO_STATE was sent and the transaction did not complete - read the mailbox error beside this line"
    Case 6
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: GET_GPIO_STATE completed but the firmware rejected pin 129 by echoing it back"
    Case 7
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: the pin was read; the SET_GPIO_STATE HIGH message could not be laid out in the mailbox buffer"
    Case 8
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: SET_GPIO_STATE HIGH was sent and the transaction did not complete - this is the write that powers the radio, so the mailbox error beside this line is the finding"
    Case 9
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: SET_GPIO_STATE HIGH completed but the firmware rejected pin 129 by echoing it back"
    Case 10
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: driven high; the GET_GPIO_STATE readback could not be laid out in the mailbox buffer"
    Case 11
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: driven high; the readback was sent and the transaction did not complete"
    Case 12
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: driven high; the readback completed but the firmware rejected pin 129"
    Case 13
      ProcedureReturn "wl_on: every transaction completed - the level beside this line is what the firmware says the pin is, and 1 is a powered radio"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "wl_on: SdioAssertWlOn has not been called in this run"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SdioMailboxErrorText(code) - one of mailbox.pi4's #MBX_ERR_* numbers,
;  as a sentence.
;
;  The numbers and their meanings are mailbox.pi4 lines 819-835. This
;  file does not own them and does not redefine them; it prints them,
;  because sdio.pi4 is now the file that issues property tags and a
;  refusal that arrives as a bare integer is a refusal nobody reads.
;
;  IF mailbox.pi4 EVER GAINS ITS OWN MailboxErrorText(), DELETE THIS AND
;  CALL THAT. Two tables of the same numbers is exactly the drift this
;  project has been bitten by before. This one exists only because that
;  file belongs to another worker today and must not be edited.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioMailboxErrorText(code.i)
  Select code
    Case -1
      ProcedureReturn "no failure recorded here - either the step succeeded or it was never reached"
    Case 0
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_OK - the transaction completed"
    Case 1
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_NOINIT - MailboxInit() was not called, or it failed"
    Case 2
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_ADDRESS - the buffer sits above 1 GiB and the VideoCore cannot name it"
    Case 3
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_TOOBIG - the value buffer this tag asked for does not fit"
    Case 4
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_TXTIMEOUT - mailbox 1 stayed FULL; the firmware is not draining it"
    Case 5
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_RXTIMEOUT - mailbox 0 stayed EMPTY; the firmware never answered"
    Case 6
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_ECHO - the reply named a different buffer, so it answers somebody else's request"
    Case 7
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_STATUS - the firmware marked the whole message an error rather than SUCCESS"
    Case 8
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_TAG - the transport worked, the message came back, and this tag's own response bit was left clear. Usually that means the firmware received the tag and did not service it - but see AN OPEN QUESTION in sdio.pi4: a set-type tag that legitimately returns nothing would look identical, and mailbox.pi4's per-tag check is stricter than U-Boot's."
    Case 9
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_MODE - a single-tag call was made while a multi-tag list was open"
    Case 10
      ProcedureReturn "MBX_ERR_EMPTY - a list was sent with no tags in it"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "unknown mailbox error code"
EndProcedure

; MailboxError() as captured at the rung that stopped the WL_ON ladder.
; -1 means no mailbox call failed - either the ladder finished, or it
; stopped at rung 0 before anything was sent.
Procedure.i SdioWlOnMailboxError()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnMbxErr
EndProcedure

; The direction and the level the firmware reported for WL_ON BEFORE
; this file wrote anything to the pin. -1 for either means that read did
; not happen. Direction follows the expander driver's enum, lines 22-23:
; 0 is RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_IN and 1 is RPI_EXP_GPIO_DIR_OUT.
Procedure.i SdioWlOnDirectionFound()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnDirFound
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioWlOnLevelFound()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnLevelFound
EndProcedure

; SET_GPIO_CONFIG is advisory - see the block above SdioAssertWlOn.
; 1 if the firmware honoured it, 0 if it did not, -1 if it was not
; reached. SdioWlOnConfigError() is MailboxError() straight afterwards.
Procedure.i SdioWlOnConfigOk()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnCfgOk
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioWlOnConfigError()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnCfgErr
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE THREE RESPONSE LENGTHS, and why they are worth a bench run.
;
;  Each is MailboxRespBytes() captured immediately after one set-type
;  tag: the length the firmware wrote into that tag's own code word.
;  They exist to settle AN OPEN QUESTION in the header without anybody
;  having to guess.
;
;    length 0 beside #MBX_ERR_TAG   the firmware returned nothing, so a
;                                   cleared response bit may simply mean
;                                   "no payload" rather than "refused" -
;                                   and mailbox.pi4's per-tag check,
;                                   which its own header records as
;                                   stricter than U-Boot's, would be
;                                   reporting a success as a failure.
;
;    length 8 beside #MBX_ERR_TAG   the firmware wrote its documented
;                                   two words and STILL left the bit
;                                   clear. That is not the empty-reply
;                                   case and explanation two would not
;                                   cover it.
;
;    length 8 beside MBX_OK         serviced, plainly. This is what
;                                   SET_CLOCK_STATE is expected to show,
;                                   and if it does then set-type tags
;                                   are not rejected as a class here.
;
;  -1 means the tag was never sent in this run.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i SdioWlOnConfigRespBytes()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnCfgResp
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioWlOnSetRespBytes()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_wlOnSetResp
EndProcedure

Procedure.i SdioClockSetRespBytes()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_clkSetResp
EndProcedure

; What SET_CLOCK_STATE replied with, and the mailbox error captured
; immediately after it. A reply of -1 means the transaction never
; completed, so the error code beside it is the reason.
Procedure.i SdioClockSetReply()
  ProcedureReturn sdio_clkSetReply
